insert into public.chunks (id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length)
select id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length
from jsonb_to_recordset($jsonpayload$
[{"id": "bdbd2d6e-a580-45eb-9d9c-7b9bc039b206", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Ian Coble (20:54): Yeah. Is it important to a lot of people? Maybe not. I think there's probably a small niche of professionals out there where this does matter, but if you're in any industry that has content production, which I'm sorry, now it's every industry under the sun, it doesn't matter what you do if you're marketing, you are in the content production industry now, whether you're doing an in-house or whether you're hiring external vendors and freelance people to do that for you. So yeah, I think it's really important to know these things. It is important because everyone is creating content now and every job is now, if you're in marketing of any sort, you're going to have to know, you're going to have to understand the ins and outs of licensing. And from a brand's perspective, I think they need to understand it because I think you always need to respect and work with the people that you are contracting with. So if you're in-house somewhere and you're in marketing, you need to understand licensing because the people that are working for you, that's how they make their livelihood. And it's important to understand and respect that. I think from a creative's perspective, from a content producer's perspective, it's super important because that's your livelihood. If you don't understand licensing, your leaving money on the table that you are rightfully legally entitled to, and why would you do that?\n\nCole Heilborn (22:28): You say that they're rightfully entitled. How so?\n\nIan Coble (22:33): Well, if I take a photo the moment I click the trigger that image registers, I own the copyright of that. You can get nerdy and yes, you have to register that copyright with the Library of Congress in order for it to have enforceability. But regardless that copyright is mine, I own that original work that it's mine.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:00): Even if you're out on assignment for a client,", "token_estimate": 477, "speakers": ["Ian Coble", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "20:54", "end_timestamp": "23:00", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "230b3037-f391-497a-a6e2-d5babcf0c02b", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Ian Coble (23:03): Unless it's a work for hire contract, yes. If I'm on assignment for a company, all of the paperwork that I have will say copyright to all images pursuant to this agreement, remain the sole and exclusive property of photographer. And unless it's specifically stated otherwise, yes, I own that anything created under there, so if they're using it, I in turn should be compensated for it. And it goes back to that model we were talking about. If it's going to get used a lot, shouldn't that person get compensated a lot? And I think about photos that you've seen over the course of your life who when they were in college didn't see the Bob Marley legend portrait of him smoking a joint that adorned every dorm room wall or the Nirvana album cover, nevermind with the baby floating and trying to swim towards the dollar bill. Those images are on, they're on billboards, they're on. (24:04): They've been used millions and millions and millions and millions of times again and again and again and again. It got used so much because it was an amazing photo and spoke to the people using it. Shouldn't the photographer be compensated for that? So under that model, I think they do, and as that's how I make my living is if it's getting used, I should be compensated based on the amount that it is getting used. If I sell that copyright in a work for hire situation where all of a sudden I take my photo and the company that hired me now has it, they can use it until the cows come home and I don't get to see a dime of that. I get the creative fee that I would've already charged anyways, but none of the residual licensing off of that. So in a fairness argument, how is that fair to a creative to not be compensated for the value of the work they created\n\nCole Heilborn (25:08): That argument ever. Does that ever get hard to communicate? Do certain people not understand that? And if so, why?", "token_estimate": 477, "speakers": ["Ian Coble", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:03", "end_timestamp": "25:08", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "6dd86281-6a29-4cb2-bac9-74dc332d8890", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Ian Coble (25:18): I don't think it's that people don't understand it. I think it's a pretty easy argument to understand it's supply and demand. The more supply there is for an image, well, I guess supply and demand is the wrong word, but value I guess would probably be a better analogy there. The more it's used, the more it's sought after, the more value it has. So I think clients understand that value in the license and in it they don't like it because they don't want to have to. And going back to what we were saying, marketing budgets haven't gone up, not relative to the number of needs that they have. They're having to stretch their budget thinner and thinner on more photo shoots, on more video shoots on more and more and more and more content. So having to come back on a successful image two years later and renegotiate an additional license on something that they feel they've already paid for, it doesn't look good on their budget. (26:24): And so they push back. And you see a lot of big brands now where they will try and force a work for hire contract in anytime they're working with a photographer. And I've had definite clients where the first contract that comes across the table, they're like, okay, we want you to shoot. Can you shoot Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday? Yeah, I can. Okay, perfect. Here's all the details. Okay, we're going to send you over a contract and I'll get halfway through the contract and tucked in there is work for hire and it's like exit out, send it back and be like, we need a new contract. And I've had certain companies be like, oh yeah, sorry. We have to try and push this. We're trying to keep our costs down. It feels like they're kind of trying to sneak it in sometimes other times they'll straight up call it out like, Hey, this doesn't work for hire, are you okay with that? And it's like, well, no, I'm not, me personally, no I'm not. I have had probably I'd say the three, maybe four clients over the course of my career that have just been, we only do work for hire period. This is all we do. And I have said no to all, but a couple of 'em. I think in certain instances it's real hard to just have a black and white argument for anything. (27:52): The two instances I think I've ever done work for hire, one of 'em got me an incredible opportunity to shoot something I never would've had in my entire life. And the ability to utilize that in my marketing and to be able to have that in my portfolio was something, and the compensation was high enough where it was like, okay, I don't feel like this is a horrible deal, but I was charging more for a work for hire than I would've had it just been a regular license, creative fee and license with it. They still paid me more for it and I had to negotiate the rights to use it in my portfolio because they technically owned it and I didn't own it anymore. I had to fight in order to be able to even say, Hey, I did shoot this even though I don't own it anymore. And there's certain instances and there's other instances where it's like if someone really wants work for hire, they really want to own those images and they're willing to pay a crap load of money for it if I have no ability to license it down the road somewhere else and they want to pay me more for it. It's not ideal, but if it's that important to, I'm sure.", "token_estimate": 834, "speakers": ["Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "25:18", "end_timestamp": "25:18", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "cf793c77-2738-4edc-861b-263c5673fe45", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (29:03): So I feel like one argument that a brand could make against the idea of licensing is kind of what you mentioned is that, so if a brand hires you to go out and shoot, let's say you're shooting a running shoe company, they come out with their models, you shoot stuff, you shoot their models running through the city, and then could you then take those photos if you own the originals and then license those photos to another brand or sell them to a stock site?\n\nIan Coble (29:33): What you're asking is a really good question, and this is where the specifics of licensing becomes so important and where it's important for everyone to understand this that's working in this world is it's all in the details. So if someone has me shoot something, yeah, there's a moral problem with someone paying you to shoot something for them and you licensing them to use that, but then licensing that to maybe even one of their competitors and you can see a real conflict of interest there, and that's where it's okay, you have either exclusivity to the images or you have industry exclusivity to the images where I can't sell to a competitor, but say your analogy of a running shoe, but what if the location that we were shooting was some hotel in Los Angeles or something and that hotel's like, wow, we really like this. Can we use this? (30:36): And it's like that's not a competitor. In fact, there's probably more value if that company, if that hotel is using those running shoes in our marketing and I can also make an additional profit off of this. Yes. But again, that goes back to the licensing. Does that company want to pay exclusive rights where no one else can use them and for how long do they have those exclusive rights or is it industry exclusive or do they even care at the same time? Again, using your analogy, if I shot for the running shoe company and say it was Brooks, Nike's not going to go buy those photos, it's a competitors. The industry exclusive is almost a moot point because people aren't going to be using their competitors' images. If they're using it, it's because they're not in the same industry.", "token_estimate": 535, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "29:03", "end_timestamp": "29:33", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "c257f695-021f-466c-b07a-6b3ce760146f", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (31:28): Yeah, interesting. So I've also heard the argument that some photographers are just, I don't remember the exact words, but they're just complaining about how the industry is changing and maybe they can't charge as much for what they once were able to. I guess thoughts on the flexibility of the industry and does that play a role in this?\n\nIan Coble (31:48): I mean, everyone likes to complain. I think the longer that you've done anything, the more you're probably going to complain about it as well because you're going to remember the good old days and how it used to be and goddamn whipper snappers that are coming in and changing things. So could you say that this is people complaining? You can Absolutely. You can say anything. The model is changing as we stated, and it's been changing for a long time, but just because it's changing doesn't in my opinion, mean that you have to change with it because every one of my contracts, or I should say probably 95% of my contracts still have licensing terms associated with them. It is very rare that I don't have a licensing contract associated with any shoot that I'm doing. It's just the way that I do business and for the newer generation that's coming up, the new content creators that are coming in, I think it's important for them to learn the history because it only serves their best interest. (33:01): If you're new and you come in, you've only been shooting a couple of years and brands are approaching you and they're sliding that contract across the table that says work for hire, and you're like, sure, you just left tons of money on the table because they're not going to tell you that, oh, by the way, you could be making a lot more money here. They're not going to tell you that. But to educate yourself and to realize the value in what you're creating, the value that the industry even knows is there. If we don't educate that up and coming generation, they don't know and it's going to make it increasingly harder and harder and more difficult to fight for the ability to charge for what an image is worth or what anything is worth.", "token_estimate": 532, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "31:28", "end_timestamp": "31:48", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "4d252546-f539-419f-b4f6-ae98c3f9e1dd", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (33:51): Do you feel like the value of photography is changing?\n\nIan Coble (33:57): Yes and no. I think back in the day, the hero image, the one shot that was just the end all, be all that a brand built its reputation and marketing around, I think those days are probably done. There's just too many uses out there that if a company took that, I mean it doesn't even matter how insane the photo or video was. If you see it on Facebook, on Twitter, on Instagram, on their website, you saw it on buses, you saw it, you'd just be like, enough already. Is this all you have? You need fresh imagery, you need multiple images, you need a cohesive set of assets to tell that story. So yeah, the days of just one image and a photographer charging hundreds of thousands of dollars for that one image, they still might come around, but I'm not holding my breath for 'em. But that doesn't mean the value of photography has gone down. If anything, I think the value of photography, and every time I say photography, I also mean video. I mean art, I mean anything graphic design, I just say photography because that's where the bulk of my work is. (35:21): There's so much need for it. There's more usage opportunities than there ever has been, and there are more getting developed every single day, and it's all visual based. People aren't stopping scrolling on their phone when they come to a story. They're going from picture to picture to picture or video to video to video driving traffic. That's what's driving interest. So I think the demand for photography is bigger than it's ever been, and the need for great amazing content is higher than it's ever been and the ability to create crazy work that wasn't even possible to create 20 years ago, it's mind blowing. And there are more companies out there than there used to be as well. There's more money out there spending money on these things, but there's also a lot more people trying to make a living on it. At the same time,", "token_estimate": 497, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "33:51", "end_timestamp": "33:57", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "3d4e583b-44ea-4599-9518-78dabc2f41c8", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (36:18): For the folks who are in-house with a marketing team or an agency and they're trying to figure out, I've got a limited pool of money, I still want to be respectful of the photographer that we're hiring, but I'm trying to pick and choose where do I put my money? I guess any suggestions for them if they're kind of in this sticky situation where they really need as many rights as they can get, but they only have so much money. Is there anything that I guess any tips, any thoughts for them?", "token_estimate": 129, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "36:18", "end_timestamp": "36:18", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "5446e74a-ce93-4614-b4c2-ef0e227048df", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Ian Coble (36:53): I think everything is case specific and it's relationship based as well. And if someone's in that situation where they're in marketing, they're in a brand and they're having budget constraints and they need, that's where you really lean on your relationships and reach out to your creators, reach out to your photographers or your videographers that you like working with and guys, we're in a pinch right now. We have this much money and we have to get these campaigns shot. What do you think? Is this an unrealistic ask? Do I need to go back to my CFO and request more funds? Maybe I need to do that next year, but is there a way to work with me this year? It's all about relationships I think at that point in time and the importance of those, and I've definitely done work for brands that have been in those situations and it's like I wouldn't just be doing that to any Joe Schmo that called me up on the phone, didn't even know who I was, and Hey, Ian, we want you to shoot with us, but we only have 50% of what the budget should actually be. (38:07): You willing to do that? For us, it's probably like, sorry, probably need to find another daughter. But if it's been someone that I've been working with for 10 years and they come at me and they're like, Hey, we have this one use or this one campaign that needs to be shot and this is how much we have, but we have a feeling it's not probably in line with what you would normally charge us, what do you think? I'm willing to work then and talk and come up with a strategy, and it doesn't mean I'm going to give the work away, but it's like, okay, well let's dig more into what do you actually need? Do you actually need a hundred photos or do you really need like 25? Because there's got to be a little bit of a give and take in that relationship. You scratch my back, I'll yours and I get it. If you're in that instance, you've got people in other departments that are saying, well, we have to have these images, but sometimes maybe that means you have to go back to them and say, we don't have the money for that many images, so unless we can get some of your budget to compensate for that, this is all we can do. (39:13): And having a good person to work with, having that network of people to rely on, it's huge to be able to come to terms with budget limitations.", "token_estimate": 586, "speakers": ["Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "36:53", "end_timestamp": "36:53", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "4074c948-e5f8-4d86-8d40-c5b0498d19aa", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (39:25): Where do you feel like things are going?\n\nIan Coble (39:32): In what sense? Give me a little bit more of a, that could go so many different directions.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:38): Fair enough. Where do you feel like licensing and this idea of work for hire is going in the future? Will it become more of a thing, less of a thing?\n\nIan Coble (39:50): I think the future is going to, the bigger a company is, the more that legal gets involved with contracts and it makes sense. That's the legal legal's department is to make sure the contracts or boilerplate, they're going to see a possibility of being liable in a non-work for hire contract. So the legal department is the more they're going to push for work for hire, the bigger the brand is, the bigger the legal department is. So the bigger brands get the more big companies that start buying up small companies and operating them under one umbrella, we're going to see a lot more work for higher in the future, but that doesn't mean that it has to be rammed down our throat because as I've said, I've only had a couple of times where in 18 years where I've had to either walk away or take it. (40:53): Just because someone wants you to do it doesn't mean you have to. So while I think that's going to be increasingly more common to see contracts that are worked for hire, it's up to my generation to try and push back on that. It's up for the newer generation to hopefully do some research, talk to the senior generation to ask for help to reach out. And I think that's a really incredible thing. I will always talk to aspiring artists and I'm not going to tell them how to do their job. I'm not going to tell them everything because at the end of the day, they are my competition, but still educating them is incredibly important because if I don't tell them and they're coming to me for help, then who is going to help them? And not helping them means they don't have the knowledge they need and they could be undercutting the industry, not just me, but the entire industry.", "token_estimate": 513, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "39:25", "end_timestamp": "39:50", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "9ff8a55a-785b-4ace-8878-0394a6517697", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (41:54): So yeah, that's something I've always heard the argument that if the new generation of photographers, filmmakers start changing their pricing models and undercutting the old way of doing it, does it actually change the industry? Does it actually change things?\n\nIan Coble (42:13): I think it's a yes and no. If enough people do it, yeah, it's going to generate a critical mass where it becomes the norm, but there's also going to be the artists that have been around forever that are just so good at what they do that brands are still going to work with them on their terms, even though it goes against what all the new generation is doing or what has the critical mass. If there's still that group of people that are just really good at what they do and they're sticking to their guns, they're going to do it. I mean, if you go to a really amazing restaurant and they're like, it's $400 a plate, you're like, God, I'm not going to spend that. And they're like, okay, well then you're not going to get this plate. And you're like, I really want that plate though. That food's amazing. Then if you really want it, you're going to pay for it. (43:13): Excellence will always be able to demand a premium, but for mediocre isn't super unique. That fills a need, but it's not crazy different. It's not something that anyone else can't do. Yeah, I could see that possibly getting pushed into a royalty or a work for hire scenario where it's just like, okay, well you're not willing to do it. There's 20 other people over here that will for this flat fee that won't charge, won't charge me licensing fees. They'll happily take the flat fee. We'll never have to deal with them again. And that's it. And I think that's where you get into a bigger conversation of not just the importance of licensing, but as an artist, the importance of being unique, the importance of having a viewpoint that is very you that no one else can do. And when you have an artist that is just, you can see their work from across the room and you're like, I know exactly who did that. It screams of their style, then brands are going to be willing to, they're like, okay, you're not going to work for hire with us. We still want to work with you. Okay, but if your work, yeah, I could see you getting pushed into work for hire five, 10 years down the line. It could just be one of those things.", "token_estimate": 597, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "41:54", "end_timestamp": "42:13", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "fbdfdeef-72df-46f7-acc2-e4bc379dc145", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (44:44): You mentioned that you had some mentors that you learned some of this stuff from back in the day. For people who are trying to learn more about licensing, do you have any resources that you'd recommend?\n\nIan Coble (44:58): Gosh, off the top of my head, I don't know if the book still exists, so I wish I'd done my research and maybe I can possibly look at it, but there was a book by the A SMP, which is the American Society of Media Photographers called Basic Principles and Stock Photography, I believe. And it's a really boring read, but it cuts into everything from contracts to invoicing to licensing, why it's important, all of those things. If that book's still not in publication, if you went to the ASP's website, they would have resources there. There's other forms out there like editorial photographers. (45:41): There's groups out there. If you go to a camera shop or something and you're a new shooter and you're buying your first cameras and you're like, I'm trying to be a professional, are there any local groups or local chapters that I can meet with? They're going to have a resource list of people that you could talk to or going to the art school. I still get hit up by the professors of like, Hey, would you be willing to have an internship for this particular photographer or would you be willing to talk to 'em? And I always will. I don't do internships anymore. That just became too difficult to manage, but I'll still always make the time for it. (46:24): Don't sell yourself short just on the power of reaching out of who's your favorite photographer on Instagram, drop 'em a dm. If you've got questions and you just like how this person's doing it, ask 'em what's the worst thing that they're going to do? Not answer you, oh, well, but the best thing they could do, maybe they get back to you and they answer some of your questions and you're never going to know unless you try. And for me, the way that I did that, and you have to also, when you're doing this, think of what you're asking. If you're in those shoes, you're asking someone that you don't know to take time out of their day, away from their job, away from their family, away from whatever they were doing in order to help you, a stranger out. So if that's the case, what value can you bring to the table for them? (47:17): When I was doing this, basically I interned and I interned for free. I went to photographers that this was before social media, so I literally just opened up the phone book a photographer and would call their office and be like, do you have room for an intern? I'm like, well, no, we don't have an internship. I'm like, can I sweep the floors? I just want to learn. Can I carry your camera bag? You don't have to pay me. And eventually some of those turned into paid side jobs while I was getting started, but I knew that if I was going to need a professional's time, I also had to be giving something in return for that. You're not going to get an education from someone that's been doing it for two, three decades. If you're just like, Hey, can you show me the keys to the kingdom? But all of a sudden, if you're doing a lot of favors for them and you're helping them out and you're creating value, then all of a sudden there's a reason they want to help you out.", "token_estimate": 826, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "44:44", "end_timestamp": "44:58", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "dc9a7cbb-41e6-45cc-bb1d-f44f4b913cb9", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (48:19): Is there anything we didn't cover that you think is relevant to address? We're kind of coming up on the end of our time for today.\n\nIan Coble (48:26): I'm trying to think of anything that I can think of. I want to go back and just say, there's a million examples of why you should license case studies on why it's important to, but at the end of the day, you can give a million case studies, and it still goes back to if you believe that the more something gets used, the more the artists that created it should be paid, then it doesn't matter the number of instances than case studies that I bring up. That's just a simple, you believe it or you don't. And I think, as I said, it's going to become more and more prevalent that we see work for higher contracts just because the way I see the world shifting is Amazon is sort of the way things are going. It's pushing under all the mom and pops, it's pushing under big corporations, big box stores now because people just can't compete. (49:29): As big, huge corporations start taking over and everything is under these huge umbrellas. The contracts that creatives see are going to be more right, stripping more all inclusive work for hire contracts where we're forced to leave things on the table. And you can either choose to accept that and charge accordingly because they're now getting a significant value and not just leaving that money on the table or fighting for it and saying, no, I'm going to stick to what I know is right, that what I do has intrinsic value or you wouldn't be coming to me. (50:21): And as such, it deserves to be compensated for. So I just think it's really a matter of from whatever side, whoever's listening to this, whether it is someone in-house, understanding the value in it and why that is important and why it's important to respect the people who are artists and why they're going fight. It's not they're greedy. I mean, maybe some people are, that's not why I'm doing it. This is how I make my living and the only way I can make my living is by charging appropriately for the work that's being done. So hopefully someone that's in-house can understand that. And then if someone's listening to this, that's an artist themselves, they can understand that, yeah, actually I shouldn't be taking those jobs if I see them come across the desk. If I do see that verbiage written in the contract, I should push back and I should have confidence to say, no, I can't do this. Or if they're like, Hey, you have to do this, and like, okay, well it's going to cost you 500% more. It's going to be a five time markup from what it otherwise would've be. And a lot of times companies will pay that then if they can't do work that isn't going to work for hire case, they're willing to pay for it, but don't just sign it because you think you have to.", "token_estimate": 712, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "48:19", "end_timestamp": "48:26", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "9be512b0-70d2-4b01-a11f-d58530961132", "episode_id": "08fa0c58-91ef-45bd-b7d3-96924819e3ca", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4874", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (51:41): Yeah, well said. If people have any questions about any of this stuff, can they find you somewhere to bug you?\n\nIan Coble (51:50): I'll watch the DM box, just blow up. Yeah, you can find me. My website, ian cobol.com, also on Instagram at Ian cobol. I think those are probably the two best ways to reach out to me. And like I said, also, I'm not the expert here. I'm just one guy. There are other people out there, and I think I've had round table discussions with other photographers and it's always an interesting thing to get multiple people's perspectives on this thing because everyone is an independent business doing this. They're doing their own thing. There isn't a price fixing thing going on out here where we all sit together and say, that photo costs $1,000 and we're all going to charge the exact same for it. So getting the opinion or the help from multiple people, it just gives you a better understanding of the scope, the scale that is out there, and then you can find where you want to operate within that scale.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:00): Beautiful. Well, Ian, I thank you for taking the time to chat with me today. This is a lot of fun, so much. No problem. This is an important topic. Probably could be a pretty boring topic if you start to get into the legal jargon behind all of this, but\n\nIan Coble (53:16): Hopefully we can make it too boring today. So\n\nCole Heilborn (53:18): No, I don't think so. I think mean there's no one way to do it. There's a whole bunch of different ways to consider this and how to approach it, and it sounds like it really comes down to the individual and their business and how they want to run their business and what they want to do and who they want to be in the market. But I think understanding where things have come from and what's pushing some of these changes is really interesting to hear. So I appreciate you breaking all this down.\n\nIan Coble (53:45): Absolutely. Thanks for sitting down with me.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:48): No problem. Alright, well I'll let you get back to your day and have a good, sounds like you got some shoots coming up, so good luck.\n\nIan Coble (53:57): Thank you, I appreciate it. Have a great day.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:58): See you. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 613, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Coble"], "start_timestamp": "51:41", "end_timestamp": "53:58", "podcast_index": 78, "episode_name": "EP 78: Ian Coble | Licensing Rights and Photography | Ian Coble Photography", "slug": "ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-78-ian-coble-licensing-rights-and-photography-ian-coble-photography", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": "Ian Coble", "categories": [], "short_description": "How to navigate licensing rights as a photographer", "podcast_length": "44:100"}, {"id": "70121621-0972-4d8c-887f-d265bd32d191", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Tom Moreno (00:00): How do we go through those journeys, those core journeys and make it a more optimized experience? How are we addressing consumer's needs? How are we suggesting once they've committed to the right product, how are we suggesting the right other products that are within our catalog that might improve their experience as well? How do we make sure that they know these are the things that you need to go do this? If you're say, a more recreational type persona to here are the things that our premier athletes, our Francoise Haynes, our Coordin to Walters, our Cody Townsends, here are the products that they use to do all of the amazing things that they do, and really talking to those personas in a different way, but making sure that the journey is consistent, recognizable, and doesn't feel forced.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:54): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn. Today I'm sitting down with Tom Marino. He is the senior manager of site experience for Solomon North America. Tom, how are you?\n\nTom Moreno (01:03): I'm doing well. How about yourself? Cole?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:05): Doing great. Yeah, really well. I'm super excited to have you on the show and super excited to jump into our conversation. How are you doing?\n\nTom Moreno (01:13): Good, thanks for having me. Yeah, excited to be here.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:15): I'd love if you could give us a quick introduction. Where are you're calling in from? How's your day going?\n\nTom Moreno (01:21): You bet. Yeah. I am in Ogden, Utah, which is about 45 minutes. Salt Lake, north of Salt Lake. We are the HQ for North America for the brand Solomon, and obviously there's a few sister brands under the Armor sports portfolio, atomic or Terets, et cetera, that have a few in place here as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:41): Gotcha. And are things pretty busy these days coming up on the end of the year?\n\nTom Moreno (01:45): Yeah, it's Q4, push holiday season. Lots of inventory out in the market's going to be pretty interesting q, Q4 in the old digital space.", "token_estimate": 514, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:00", "end_timestamp": "01:45", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "ce83976d-fca6-4c62-9444-8c88c3a78f94", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:55): So senior manager of site experience. We've obviously had the chance to connect prior. I know a little bit about what that title means. I'd love if you could break down what exactly does that mean? What does your day-to-day look like? What do you get to work on?\n\nTom Moreno (02:08): Yeah, it's nice and nice and vague, but what does it really mean? It's really the intersection of site operations and digital product development. It's really thinking through how do we bring a physical product onto the website for the North American consumer, and then how are we building a website journey that's better consumer, whether they're coming for a more technical product or maybe it's a more comfort oriented or benefit driven product. That's really what we do.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:38): Gotcha. And when you say you're at the intersection between, sorry, did you say design and ux? Yeah, so\n\nTom Moreno (02:45): It's site operations on one side, so that's like putting up banners, putting up the catalog, all the traditional site merchandising pieces, and then digital product development. So looking at say, a search function or a cross sell tool, maybe that's a third party tool, maybe it's internally developed and looking at how consumers are behaving with it, what kind of benefit is it driving to the business and then looking at refining it as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:10): Gotcha. And on a typical day to day, what are you working on? Are you coding? Are you designing things? Are you doing neither of those things?", "token_estimate": 386, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "01:55", "end_timestamp": "03:10", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "bbc18fe6-6c83-4feb-9143-49033d5ad8e6", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Tom Moreno (03:21): No coding, a little bit of design, but more so on the wire frame. So I might look at say, designs or mockups with actual UX designers, user interface designers might be working with a product owner or product manager on talking about use cases. Could be talking with engineers about chasing bugs that are happening, filing those tickets, working to flip switches in the backend to say put something live. But really looking at site calendar, what are the things that are coming in the future? What are we prepping for? What are the visuals that we have? What is the copy that we're putting up? Getting all those pieces in place. And then obviously all the additional product stuff I just mentioned.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:02): Gotcha. And what does the team breakdown look like? What does your team consist of and what are the teams that you're working with look like?\n\nTom Moreno (04:10): Yeah, so the site experience team has, there's two other guys on my team. There is a site merchandiser, so visual specialist if you will, and then there's a site operations slash site optimization associate as well. And he's really focused on what are the sort orders on our product listing pages. So if you go to men's shoes or women's shoes, what are the products in the order? What is the logic that we're using behind that is automated? Are we pinning things? Maybe say we have a big campaign activation, putting that product specifically in the first spot, things like that. And then I also work in conjunction with our global product team. So there's a global merchandising team that works through creating larger campaign assets, something that is more globally relevant that we may be activating at the same time as our counterparts in EMEA. And then we also work with the digital product team, which is over there. And that is again, looking through all of those different consumer journeys and what are the behaviors that we wanted to encourage better consumer journey behavior.", "token_estimate": 499, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:21", "end_timestamp": "04:10", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "d7435c6e-3389-4f89-9493-8c51fe2f92e6", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (05:14): Interesting. So I feel like it's 2022. There's so many things to talk about when it comes to marketing and social media and branding and content and YouTube. But one thing that I don't hear a lot of people talking about is websites and well, frankly, kind of the backbone of a lot of that content, which is one of the reasons why I'm really excited to have you on the show just to dive into your world and some of the things that you're noticing and some of the things that maybe some people aren't aware of when it comes to websites and optimizing them and increasing user flow and increasing consumer experience on them. I'm curious if you could take a step back and if you could define what is the goal for Solomon with your website? Is there one goal? Are there multiple goals? How do you think about that?", "token_estimate": 209, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:14", "end_timestamp": "05:14", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "e7ca7270-0a1e-4253-98c3-12a62f1c3535", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Tom Moreno (06:06): Yeah, that's a good one. I think when you look at the consumer journey, it's all about what are they coming to your website for, right? There's so many digital touch points that are out there, social media, content pieces like YouTube, but really that insight is an education piece. There's obviously other retailer websites out there that can help do those things. So our goal is really to, and this has been established I would say more so in the last year, and the way that we're refining the website is really build a sport specialist. We have a pretty wide catalog when it comes to playing outdoors. Obviously in North America, the brand is really well known. If you're a skier or a snowboarder or you cross country ski, if you are say a runner or maybe you're into sneakers, maybe you don't know about the brand as much, maybe you're a sneakerhead and you like the swoosh or you like the three stripes and those are the brands that you're really into. (06:58): The brand awareness there is a little bit different. So the consumer needs are going to be a little bit different than say, consumers who already know what your products are. So it's really about looking at how are we inspiring consumers when they come to the website, how do they understand what the brand is and then understand the brand through that category lens. And there are different regional articulations for some of those things based on product preferences with skiing probably being the most stark. So it's inspire, it's how do we guide people? What are the right products for this consumer based on their persona? Are they a more committed athlete? Are they somebody that is out there training every day? Are they somebody out there on the skin track every morning? What are their motivations? What are their needs? They probably know a little bit more about the products they want. Maybe it's a more technical specification that they're looking for looking, or is it a more recreational type consumer or is it someone who is really looking for specific benefits to do a specific activity? Their motivations, their needs are different and may be more benefits driven here and it may be more technical driven there.", "token_estimate": 554, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "06:06", "end_timestamp": "06:06", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "f177f64e-ed2d-4eac-aac1-9218ccff3327", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (08:04): So you talk about articulation and variations in the website for users in regions and with seasons. What does that actually look like? If I go to the website today, how does that differ from six months from now?\n\nTom Moreno (08:19): I would say let's just start with the today and juxtaposing it with Europe versus the us. There are some pretty stark differences there. So skiing as an example may be really what would be called on piece. So really resort focused skiing. It's a more rental oriented culture, whereas in North America, the brand is really known for its free rate products, the QST, the stance lines, those are really what people are using to ski all over the mountain. And the visuals that go along with that are different. So it may be a stance campaign as an example in the two regions, but the imagery that you're going to use to speak to those are going to be a little bit different. And maybe I'm skiing on a groomed run, maybe I'm skiing in chopped up crut, same thing goes for trail running. It's really about where are you running on huge aspiring peaks. (09:12): The Alps is an insanely beautiful place and there's great amazing places here, but the trail running is a little bit different here as far as where you're going to go and what is more approachable. It's really thinking through those types of things. And then your question about six months from now, it's really about seasonality. So making sure that you have imagery that matches the season with whatever you're using. So if I'm six months from now and it's the end of ski season, things are tailing down, maybe that's more spring skiing orientation, maybe it's touring, et cetera. And then on the trail run side, maybe it's a little bit warmer and it's not winter trail running.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:50): How customized can you get with that? If I'm up here in the northwest, if I go to the website today, can you show me only ski products? But if I was living in Arizona, maybe you would only be showing me trail running products today. Is that possible", "token_estimate": 517, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "08:04", "end_timestamp": "09:50", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "da3dbb0f-d332-4250-8511-a06ca31e1b71", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Tom Moreno (10:06): The way that our site is set up today? No, there are definitely tools out there that allow you to personalize based on those things. I would say it's definitely something we're exploring, but the sexy stuff, the personalization, things like that, they're really, really hard and you have to have a lot of the fundamentals in place for that to matter. So product information management systems, photo management systems, content management systems, all those things have to be in place. And then you have to be very good about curating the recipes, if you will, about, okay, what am I actually targeting and is this consumer in Arizona actually looking for trail running gear? Or maybe they're actually a skier that's looking to go on a vacation. So you have to understand those two pieces and really, I mean it's like a retail store. You have to have fundamentals in place when they walk in to at least understand, okay, that category is there, this category is there.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:54): It seems like a lot of if then statements Oh yes, like hundreds, thousands of them.\n\nTom Moreno (11:00): Yeah, it can get pretty out of control for sure.\n\nCole Heilborn (11:03): So I'm curious, I look back at the early two thousands and websites for a while were kind of a nice to have, but maybe not a need to have or at least that was some people's perspectives and obviously they're here and they're not going anywhere. I'm curious, what does the future of what website mean to you and how does that fit into the equation of the company and their marketing?", "token_estimate": 394, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:06", "end_timestamp": "11:03", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "44f948c3-661f-42f5-ad7d-bcfe8126827d", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Tom Moreno (11:27): Yeah, cool. That's a good question. I think when you look at what are consumers interested in, it's what do they use a website for? Maybe they're coming to your website because they saw something inspirational on social media or through a marketing campaign. Maybe they're already members of your mail list and they're coming via that way. How am I inspiring them through that first page? I was going to mention this a little bit later, but when you think about where you come on the website, it's never just to the homepage. If you think about the ways that people get there, so rough metrics, usually across most sites, and this varies, but about 30% come to the homepage, 30% come to a listing page, which would be a category page, like trail running shoes or something like that. A third come to a product detail page. So looking at a specific shoe, so let's say the Ultra Glide from Solomon as an example, and those can all have different touch points, whether it's CRM, email list, text message, maybe it's a social media post. (12:35): And then the last little remainder really comes through higher search intent content pages. So if you run a Google search for how to trail run shoes or how to trail run, sorry, or how to buy trail running shoes or what are the things that I need in trail running shoes, there's content out there that is optimized to meet those search terms and there's different search volumes for each of 'em. So those are really the ways, and it's really, again, understanding what are those consumer needs, what are your consumer personas for your website, and what are the things that I'm doing to meet those needs? Again, how am I providing inspiration? How am I providing the guidance? Once I have kind of set that hook and maybe that inspiration came offsite and now I'm fully into a guidance mode. So it's benefits, it's tech specs, it's features, really the key things and really creating that immersive experience that helps a consumer imagine themselves using that product. Maybe I'm going on a hike out in Colorado and it's using that imagery that helps them say, you know what? I could actually be that person on that mountain.", "token_estimate": 547, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "11:27", "end_timestamp": "11:27", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "94671304-302c-4f90-8d89-1bd8cb48baf3", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (13:43): So when you think about all the different ways that people find themselves at your website and you think about all the different motivations as you're describing, have you guys gone through and mapped out, here's the top 100 ways that people arrive at our site, at our site and what they're looking for, and then that starts to dictate how you build the site?\n\nTom Moreno (14:06): Yeah, I mean, I would say we absolutely have gone through and it's I would say, pretty standard for all those different pieces, the specific pages, the specific types of pages. That's always going to be dynamic and fluctuate based on whatever the trends are, whatever the activations are within a given period, so, so we understand those consumer journeys where I'd say our biggest work over the next couple of years is how do we go through those journeys, those core journeys and make it a more optimized experience? How do we addressing consumer's needs? How are we suggesting once they've committed to the right product, how are we suggesting the right products that are within our catalog that might improve their experience as well? How do we make sure that they know these are the things that you need to go do this? If you're say, a more recreational type persona to here are the things that our premier athletes, our Francoise Haynes, our Courtney to Walters, our Cody Townsend, here are the products that they use to do all the amazing things that they do, and really talking to those personas in a different way, but making sure that the journey is consistent, recognizable, and doesn't feel forced.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:15): Why is it important that the journey feels consistent and not forced?", "token_estimate": 427, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "13:43", "end_timestamp": "15:15", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "88bdd968-4717-4fd2-8c96-78dac685c88f", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Tom Moreno (15:19): Well, the not forced thing is an immediate turnoff. Let's say, let's use a retail store as an example. If I walk into a retail store and the first thing that they're doing is trying to sell me something, they don't necessarily know that I'm actually there to purchase something. Maybe I'm there to look around and get intelligence about maybe say a specific product or I want to educate myself about what are the things that I need, but I'm not really right ready to purchase today. So that's how a forced interaction can push you right out of that funnel. I saw a post recently about retail shops on LinkedIn and about how consumers are coming in and they want to get fit, and then they're on their phone shopping while they're doing it, and then they'll go purchase elsewhere. In e-commerce world, that same thing exists. It's called bounce rate, it's called exit rate, and it's basically they're coming to the website, they're doing some intelligence gathering, and they may buy from you if they may buy from somewhere else. (16:16): Solomon as a brand has so many points of distribution. There's retail stores, whether that's a independent retailer, it's a larger chain to obviously our website. And at the end of the day, the consumer is going to choose based on their needs and preferences. Maybe that's convenience, maybe that's education, maybe that's some sort of loyalty program. Maybe that's some sort of affinity for the education they've got. If you look at independent retailers, some of the things that they are best at is educating the consumer about a specific product or about a specific category. We'll use boot fitting as the example. The story that I mentioned earlier was about people coming in and charging them for that time. That's putting a friction point to just come in and actually get that education as opposed to leaning into your experience say, and building some sort of loyalty, some sort of trust and standing behind your experience and your education to use that as a benefit versus a friction point, if that makes sense.", "token_estimate": 520, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "15:19", "end_timestamp": "15:19", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "c643928e-79cc-4abe-8040-5da706c387b4", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (17:18): So how do you feel about a pop-up ad asking me to subscribe to your email list when I first log into the website,\n\nTom Moreno (17:27): Not my area, but it is my area, not my favorite thing if I'm being totally honest. It is absolutely an important way to acquire new users to your email list. It also means that people are going to sign up. I mean, I do this on other people's websites. I'm sure you do as well. Sign up with whatever made up email you have just to get that discount to make that first time purchase. So that's not really building true loyalty. It's basically just saying you can have a discount of always this when you come to shop at our store, that is going to happen at retail stores as well, right? You're going to walk into a shop and you're say, okay, I want this pair of chair running shoes and maybe I'm going to have a top and a bottom, and you can ask for say, 10% discount. (18:09): Or maybe even the sales says, ask for a 10% discount. It's kind of the same kind of concept. That being said, when you pop up an interstitial like that, it does break the consumer's mindset about what they are actually trying to do on your website. It's like, oh, here, look at me. Now I'm going into this funnel. What was I here for? And sometimes that can create problems as well. So they're a necessary evil, but there are other ways to work through that. So we actually just launched our loyalty program called s plus, and I imagine as we grow that program, we will see less and less of the popups on our website when we first land.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:44): I appreciate that. So when you think about, I'm really intrigued, but you keep using the word flow and keep talking about how consumers flow through the website. Are there any specific examples that you could give of either design elements or UI elements that inhibit flow? Anything that you've seen with your time at the company or just throughout the industry?\n\nTom Moreno (19:10): Let's use Amazon as an example of a really good flow state. When you go to Amazon, how do you find a product?\n\nCole Heilborn (19:18): It's a for it.", "token_estimate": 532, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "17:18", "end_timestamp": "19:18", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "4afb82e1-4de1-4390-a43a-3f8f1337d7af", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Tom Moreno (19:19): Yeah, exactly. So what they've done is they've made search hyper primary in their shopping experience, whether you are on the website or you're on the mobile app, which are slightly different experiences, but basically blended to be the same thing. And that's getting back to that kind of consistency. But they have made sure that based on your intent, you are able to find what you need. And then the other thing, what is the other reason that you probably shop at Amazon\n\nCole Heilborn (19:47): Research trying to figure something out.\n\nTom Moreno (19:49): Okay. And then what would be one past that after you've committed to the\n\nCole Heilborn (19:54): Order price, price comparison,\n\nTom Moreno (19:56): And what's one more? What's the thing that Amazon is best at?\n\nCole Heilborn (20:00): I feel like an idiot for not having your answer.\n\nTom Moreno (20:02): No, that's okay. It's logistics. It's really about when I place this order, I know that the product is going to be at my doorstep most likely within two days and sometimes within a day, and sometimes even the same day. I was ordering coffee not that long ago, ordered it on a Saturday morning at 10:00 AM and it was literally at my door by 2:00 PM which is insane, but that is how good they are at predicting and distribution, but also making sure that last mile distribution to your house is really, really buttoned up. So it's building in that confidence level so that when I search for the product and I've found what I need, maybe I'm comparing things. I also don't even think about how long is this going to take to get there or when is it going to be there. It's just not even part of the consideration journey because that trust is built in.", "token_estimate": 433, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:19", "end_timestamp": "20:02", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "26ef5ce6-af3d-435d-b589-b3c8d5796523", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (20:50): So now you've got me thinking about the times I've spent on Amazon and how they're keeping me focused. I use this as an example in a previous episode, but I've recently bought a new mic for the podcast and I went to Amazon looking for a mic, knowing which mic I wanted to get, and I found it, I was ready to click buy, and then I noticed that I could, there's that little tab that pop was up at the bottom. It's like, oh, if you're buying this, you might also want this. And that gives two or three other options. And one of them was a boom arm for the microphone, and I was like, oh, shoot. Well, I obviously need one of those. So I ended up purchasing it, and now I'm using it today, but I'm thinking about the importance of flow, and I went there for a microphone, and only after I found the microphone did it suggest something else to compliment my purchase. Had I gone to Amazon first, looked for the microphone, and then it started throwing me other products that were peripheral products that could help me, I feel like that might've disrupted me and it might've gotten me distracted from my intent.\n\nTom Moreno (21:58): Yeah, a hundred percent. Yeah. So recommendations is really surfacing those at the right time is a good example. So we just replatformed our website. That's what I actually was signed up with Solomon to do is essentially help us replatform from an old instance of Magento to a new instance of Magento. I came in pretty late in the process during what's called UAT, user acceptance training, basically learning some new systems. They've built some internal tools for all brands to kind of manage their catalogs. And then each website is tailored and has a little bit different needs, again, addressing different consumer needs. Wilson as an example, obviously has a whole team orientation in the way that they're selling products. So kind of bulk orders if you'll, so sorry, what was the question? I lost my train of thought there.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:46): No, you were talking about flow and what you've been brought in to do recently for Solomon.", "token_estimate": 525, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "20:50", "end_timestamp": "22:46", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "70075b7f-e280-4ea9-8103-a2859b8f4065", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Tom Moreno (22:51): Great. Yeah, so Wilson is they have these team sales, and that is a different orientation. So we have the ability to curate the experience while having some common tools on the backend that allow the portfolio to have some consistency and efficiencies on the backend or synergies, if you will. And what we're really doing is trying to understand through going back again to that sports specialist, what are those different pieces for each of those? What are the target personas by region that we're trying to tailor the website to? And then add those little pieces without, again, it feeling forced. So that's your popup example or a bad recommendation example, and something that's natural, which is like your boom arm example. If you look at the way that the website is today, we are working through optimizing those pieces. So we have a cross-sell module on our product detail pages. (23:51): A really good example is we had two modules. One was a cross-sell, so we called it complete the look. This would be products you would buy with a similar thing. We first replatformed the website that was at the top of our PDP, probably the most common best practice. And what consumers are really looking for is a comparison because of this product that I'm looking at, what are other products that I might be interested in? So we AB tested that, which is called a multivariate test. We had, I think it was two different variants, basically flip-flopping those two pieces. And the one that had the better results at the cart rate, conversion rate, et cetera, was actually the similar products. So we've made that change based on the things that we saw across the regions, across volumes and the metrics that we were really looking for to try and increase that. So the next phase of that is what are the recommendations across those sports specialties that you're adding to again, make it less forced and really feel like you're a specialist.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:53): Can you share what some of the findings were between your AB testing that made you decide to go one direction or the other?", "token_estimate": 527, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:51", "end_timestamp": "24:53", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "2805b369-144f-4d62-872b-e68ddbe493bc", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Tom Moreno (25:01): Yeah, I don't remember the specifics as far as what the rates were, but the number that jumps out to me is we saw about a 20% lift in add to cart rate. So when you had that similar product, you had a higher percentage of people adding those products to cart from that product detail page. Not necessarily from those similar products, but your add to cart rate increased on those pages.\n\nCole Heilborn (25:24): Interesting. What about color? Does color play a role in flow and the user's experience and preventing roadblocks?\n\nTom Moreno (25:35): Depends. Let me ask a qualifying question. What type of color are you talking about? Are you talking about product color? Are you talking about banners? Are you talking about buttons?\n\nCole Heilborn (25:44): Yeah, everything on the site. So other than colors of product, but if we're looking at a user interface right now that's kind of a gray scale, and I dunno, to me it feels inviting and easy to use, but if this was a orange screen, I might feel a little antsy or anxious right now.", "token_estimate": 264, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "25:01", "end_timestamp": "25:44", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "16aad5d9-0d0e-4fb3-b809-eb978bab5694", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Tom Moreno (26:03): Yeah, that's a good question. So I would say overall design aesthetics each, it should match whatever your brand archetype or persona is. So that is a way to make a consumer who is coming back to you feel that kind of consistency. So there's that design piece, and there can be different design components that sit behind that. Maybe that's your typeface that you're using across different points of the website. So different font types to different banner colors. So I'll use the dropdown menu as an example. We typically will have those in black, but let's say we want to emphasize something in a given period. Maybe it's a sale, maybe it's a specific thing, a shoe finder or something like that. We can change the color of pieces like that and you will see an increase in those click-through rates on items like that. So it does play a point. (26:55): The thing you have to be careful about with color, we'll use product listing pages. An example. So I'm looking at all a bunch of shoes. If black, your bestseller in all of those shoes is the primary color, you're literally looking at a wall of black shoes. Is that the best orientation or presentation of the brand? Probably not. So there has to be some curation of that. Sometimes it can be automated again, sometimes it can be a manual and site merchandiser discretion or based on campaign activation, et cetera. Maybe you want to match the specific products to a specific banner that you have on that page for an activation that you're running.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:31): All right. I'm taking a step back a little bit earlier, you were talking about the similarities between a website and a retail store, and you've used this analogy a couple of times. Is that how you think about a website?", "token_estimate": 442, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "26:03", "end_timestamp": "27:31", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "369cc90f-7089-4389-b4a2-6e1c9048dd7b", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Tom Moreno (27:44): Me personally? Yes. I worked in a store for eight years, so I started, well, lemme step back there, or I've worked in retail for about 10 years, about a decade. I spent eight of those years at evo. I started EVO in Seattle. I started in oh six there when its store started in oh five. So I started in the receiving and worked my way up through booth fitting and ski sales and management for about eight years. For me, it is a natural orientation. So going back to the recommendation analogy that I gave you, that support specialist, that independent retailer who's really good at products, as soon as I've got that customer committed, especially if they're a really good shop of the sales training program, service training behind it, that can help back it up. They're going to talk about all of the things. Let's say I'm buying a ski set, I've already committed to boots, now I'm recommending, okay, these are the other skis and bindings that you might like with it. And guess what? We also have this service program where if you buy all three items, you're going to get the mounting for free and that's an x dollar value, et cetera. So being able to articulate in a similar way for their website, you're addressing the same consumer needs. You may not say be able to do the service online, at least today. And honestly, I don't know, in the future, I feel like that is honestly best served at retail by someone who is hands-on every single day. (29:09): You have to address those consumer needs in a similar way. You have to be able to recommend the right products. It's the same orientation, it's the same consumer journey, if you will. It's just done in a physical space versus a digital space.\n\nCole Heilborn (29:22): So obviously one challenge of the digital space is helping customers bridge that gap of not being able to touch and feel a product or try it on. How do you overcome that and are there pieces of technology that are being developed that could help with that in the future?", "token_estimate": 504, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "27:44", "end_timestamp": "29:22", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "74f811bd-cc5f-4f91-a10b-2f249ef2d079", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Tom Moreno (29:40): Yeah, yeah, definitely. So this is a pretty wide open topic. I would say that the short today is ar. That's probably the most consistent thing. I'll use sunglasses as a really good example. So if you go to Oakley's website, you go to various different websites, you're going to be able to actually just press a button on your phone and you're going to be able to try that on, try it on digitally with relatively good accuracy. There's different providers that do different orientations in different versions of it. So you want to make sure that feels as natural as possible. Obviously this is commonplace in social media, whether that's your Instagrams, tiktoks, your Snaps, et cetera. Really thinking through how do I make it feel like I'm actually not in a digital space, but I'm in a physical space. So that's one example. The other example that I've seen out there and something I was exploring when I was at evo, so we created a program called Evo Fit. (30:37): So I moved from retail management for eight years, kind hit a roadblock in my progression and joined the buying team. So I wrote a proposal, which is, I was pretty naive when I wrote this proposal, but asked the founder out to coffee. We sat down, we talked about it, and then I was brought on as an assistant buyer, took over the water sports category and then eventually led into ski and helped launch serve. But as I was managing the retail store, we built a consistent kind of booth fitting program, and this was just when the store was in Seattle, but then we built this consistent program, we called the Evo Fit. Once I moved into the buying, and it was really about educating the consumer. We'll guarantee your fit. We're going to do some of the service work for free, but you need to buy these three critical items. (31:24): The first is the boot, making sure that boot fits you. And when I say a boot fits you, there's different factors that go into it, right? It's what does the shape, what does the flex? What are your motivations? Price is absolutely a factor. So EVO obviously offers different things from past seasons that allowed people to get maybe a boot that fits the same as a current one, but at a discounted rate and different options there. So fit was we trained up the staff to make sure that we have that. The second component was a foot bed. We want to make sure your foot is in a consistent space each time. We want to make sure it's supported. And then the third is a sock. We want to make sure you're not putting on this huge thick wool sock. It completely changes the way that your booth fits. (32:04): So if you bought those three components, we would stand behind it with a fit guarantee, and there were different versions of it. And it's kind of been, I would say streamlined for Sonic because Evo sells a lot of boots in the winter. In the time that we were developing that program, I was looking at other options out there. How do we take that physical experience and merge it with the digital experience? There's a lot of different versions out there. There are companies that scan your feet, but none of 'em that usually use this, which pretty much everybody has in a way that makes it super obvious. So the blue sky vision was to take actual boot dimensions. Think of this, I was at a retailer. So trying to get those boot dimensions across all the different brands was very hard. But then what are your physical dimensions and then what are your consumer needs? (32:54): And you use those three kind of components to help provide them a recommendation, which is literally what you're doing in that retail space, but you're bringing it the technology at the time, specifically, the cameras for the mobile devices weren't there, but there are some companies out there that do some things like this. Nobody has quite found the right thing. I would not be surprised if there is something out there today where this is done at least at a vertical level, meaning it's a brand where they're giving you specific dimensions, you scan yourself, maybe it's apparel, maybe it's shoes, et cetera, and then they're giving you different recommendations. There's another couple of companies out there that do recommendations based on essentially conversions for people with products that look like you. And I would say that's probably the most common thing that I've seen out there. The problem with that is, is there are differences in the fit. So ski boots as an example, specifically on average that be your foot may be similar to them, but to get the true kind of right fit, it doesn't necessarily mean that your foot is going to fit well in that. So really the blue sky there is how do I take the physical dimensions of both the product and the person and understand their motivations? What are the preferences that I listed off earlier and give the right product recommendation based on that.", "token_estimate": 1227, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "29:40", "end_timestamp": "29:40", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "a6234b47-bbb5-44ad-980f-4dde1845ea76", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (34:16): About a year ago, I was looking at a tent on REI, and you're talking about the ar. I could pop the tent up in my living room and\n\nTom Moreno (34:27): Walk\n\nCole Heilborn (34:27): Around it.\n\nTom Moreno (34:27): All the furniture websites do that. Amazon actually has this on quite a few product pages as well for furniture, et cetera. I would say the ar, see it in your spot. Home Depot does this as well. This is random, but I was looking at a flange for a toilet the other day, and if I wanted to look at the toilet flange in my bathroom, I could look at the toilet flange, which is, I don't know who's doing that, but it's definitely a possibility that you could do,\n\nCole Heilborn (34:56): If I'm using an AR tool, and let's say I'm an average consumer, I realize this is probably very dependent upon the individual who's using that tool. How much does that help me? How much does that increase my confidence in the purchase? If I'm looking at the tent in my living room, is it 10%, 50%?\n\nTom Moreno (35:16): I mean, it depends on the person is what I would say. And it depends on what their motivations are. AR at the end of the day is going to be a very aesthetic driven thing. I mentioned earlier people trying to place themselves using that product. It's essentially the same thing, but in a slightly different orientation in a slightly different way. So it's going to depend on what is the accuracy of said thing in said space, and if it is a high level, so I'll use 3D spin, which is something we've been testing on the website, it's actually a pretty high increase depending on the product, depending on the type of user where you will see, say a 50% increase to add to cart. Now that's on one specific product and a few specific colors. So is that true across the whole product catalog? That is a different conversation, but those can have pretty high confidence builders because you're able to touch and feel it, if you will.", "token_estimate": 491, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "34:16", "end_timestamp": "35:16", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "4b7e92a9-d223-4e87-82ec-7a34307e378a", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (36:14): Alright, so with skiing, well, with any sport really, some people want to make sure that their kit is on brand, it matches their personality, the color schemes match, and if you're buying a top and a bottom, sometimes you don't know if those two will mesh well, if you're just looking at pictures of them. Could I take a photo of me on a ski hill wearing the existing kit, upload it to a site, it then swaps out the products that I'm looking at for the products that I'm currently wearing, and then I could see myself in that kit in the environment that I would be in. Is that a possibility?", "token_estimate": 154, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "36:14", "end_timestamp": "36:14", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "c2d9ce52-7aa7-4379-a42a-8c8007a3ff5a", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Tom Moreno (36:53): I mean, anything's a possibility that I would say, right? That's the first thing. And kind of the rule of development, it's really how much does it cost and what is the return you're going to get on the time and money that you're sinking into developing that, and what are the metrics that you're actually looking to move? What is the outcome that you're really looking for? So if you think of it through that lens, yeah, you could absolutely do it, but what is the total addressable market where people can do this for a brand that's just selling, say, ski? We'll, you sell as an example, the total addressable market is pretty small when you compare it to the total addressable market. For people that wear shoes, there's 330 million people roughly in the us. Pretty much all of them have at least one pair of shoes. I would say on average. (37:41): That's a pretty fair baseline. People like myself who work at a shoe company may of a little bit more than that, but how many of those people actually have tech wear outer sets that they're actually going to go skiing with? It's a much smaller percentage, I want to say between skiing and snowboarding. I haven't seen any data on this, but it was around 12 million people roughly in the US scene. That's a fraction of what is actually available for the total addressable market in the us. So differences there and being able to do that are harder. I think from an AR perspective, personally for me that doesn't feel like that good of a use case. I've seen some examples, some of the more D two C oriented brands that have really popped up the last couple of years in social media where they will essentially have a kit builder where you can essentially swap a jacket and a pan back and forth and look at the different kits that basically really consistent photo imagery. So high intent with how I'm going to do it, shooting those photos for a specific end product. And when I say product, I mean an experience online, an interface that someone can interact and have a better visual.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:51): What about vr? Does that play a role in any of this?", "token_estimate": 533, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "36:53", "end_timestamp": "38:51", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "63155189-1ba3-4f8e-b1c1-c063144070a1", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Tom Moreno (38:55): I'm not big into metaverse. NFTs is the first thing I would say. I definitely Nintendo, what was it called? Virtual Boy I think is what it was called back in the day. So I've definitely been around the virtual experience for a long time from a kid that, for me at least for the outdoor industry, feels like a tough sell. We want to be outdoors more on our screens, less, we're on a screen right now. I think both of us would probably rather be, skiing right now is a decent example. So I think when you think through vr, what is again the consumer needs that you're actually trying to address there? Is it actually being able to place myself in that experience? So from a marketing perspective, I think if I could throw on a VR headset and go watch Cody Townsend ski down a line, that actually to me is kind of interesting, but it's really about inspiration there. I don't really care about what products he's on. I don't really care about actually going and buying it right after it. I just want to experience what he's experiencing.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:01): Sure. So because of the seat that you have in the intersection that you work with, you work with a variety of different folks all trying to accomplish similar goals. I'm curious, when you're working with someone in the marketing department, what are some of the challenges or what are some of the opportunities that exist when you're collaborating with someone from that department as you're trying to bring a product page or some sort of a site to fruition?", "token_estimate": 385, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "38:55", "end_timestamp": "40:01", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "87346e37-bc66-4a21-8239-64a22a024077", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Tom Moreno (40:27): Yeah, I would actually start at just the overall business model. So Solomon as a brand has been wholesale since its inception within North America. We've been doing D two CA little over 10 years now, but not all categories. Winter Sports Equipment is a more recent edition where we're selling, we've been selling snowboards for a while direct to consumer. But skis and Nordic, you can find there's big supply chain challenges within that. So what's available today is literally today is a bit of a challenge and hodgepodge in what's available. But because of that, it's not necessarily that the business was built with D two C in mind. So I would say that's overall the biggest challenge is you've essentially, this is a bad analogy, but bolted on a D two C business to a wholesale infrastructure. So there's a lot of what I would consider, Evo, I think probably spoiled me in a lot of ways where they had a lot of internally built purpose driven tools that really helped them shine as a retailer, whether that was the site merchandising that they had. (41:29): Even, I'll go to just the retail example, probably my introduction to product development was I was in the receiving role at EVO at the time in the retail store, and one of our developers at the time who was also from Idaho, which is where I grew up, so we kind of bonded on that. I was telling him the way I have to print stickers to put a barcode in a price on every individual thing is I have to hand key in every single line and hand key in the quantity. And because their interface was so bad, if I made a mistake in that process, I had to start all the way over, and that could be an 80 line thing. So it was actually super easy to make those mistakes. You get pulled away to do something, and I don't even know where I'm anymore. (42:11): So we had this conversation, I showed him five minutes of what my process was. He came back the next day and he's like, Hey, try this out. And I basically pressed the button, and from the receipt that I input into our receiving system, the stickers automatically printed the exact quantities that I needed, and it saved me. I don't even know how many hours of my personal life he saved me, but I was like, oh, so that's what product development is. Yeah. So Evo had those kind of purpose built tools, obviously give you that story. If you look at the way our businesses, there are a bunch of different iterations and a bunch of different pieces with that. Probably the biggest juxtaposition that I would say for Evo versus a brand like Solomon is Evo is talking about a bunch of different brands and they're talking about products, they're talking about stories within said products across said brands. (43:05): Maybe that's their own story. Maybe it's a story that the brands are pushing. Sometimes those align, sometimes those don't. With Solomon, it's kind of the same thing, but through a more product oriented lens, there is storytelling that can happen. Maybe it's a tech story, maybe it's an initiative like WMN, maybe it's an initiative like sustainability, and those are things that you can weave through different stories. But a lot of times, and this has been really insightful for me to see, is within a brand, you have a very specific kind of set activation window, if you will, and really you're thinking about trying to maximize that demand within that window versus necessarily selling over a season. So it's much more like a spike that then tapers down, whereas a retailer may have a more kind of even keel across that specific product as an example. So because of that, on the marketing side, I think with e-commerce websites don't shut off and working with our brand team to educate them on how do we come back to that and tell that story again and again throughout the season? Because consumers don't come to the same website every single day. They may not even come the same week, they may not even come the same month. So how many times can I surface this without it feeling inauthentic or forced or repetitive?", "token_estimate": 1016, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "40:27", "end_timestamp": "40:27", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "0d629e59-05f5-4a18-994f-cff05c2062d0", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Cole Heilborn (44:25): Yeah. How do you do that? How do you think through that?\n\nTom Moreno (44:27): Yeah, that's a good question. The first thing is we built standards, right? So when I started, I would say it was a really small team, and we've reshifted the global team plays a component with it specifically on kind of merchandising best practices. They help build campaign banners, et cetera, give us guidance on different various things. But we really put in place more site calendars and then processes for how do we bring a product online for say, a specific kind of high heat event? How do we do different pieces on the website? But really working with those brand managers on say, a seasonal calendar for their categories. What are the things that we should be talking about in any given month? Some categories may only have two to four months filled in with a specific product activation, but that doesn't mean we shouldn't be talking about their category in, especially from an investment standpoint. What are the things that we want to talk about that help show us as a sports specialist throughout an entire season, right? Skis, snowboards, Nordic, maybe a tighter actual and consumer usage window, but those categories live on the website at all times. So just as an example.\n\nCole Heilborn (45:43): Interesting. Is there anything that folks who might be listening to this episode, a lot of people are in your classic marketing positions, anything that they should be aware of that you come across frequently in the position that you sit in? Any best practices or rules of thumb or just good advice when working with someone like you?", "token_estimate": 412, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "44:25", "end_timestamp": "45:43", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "12f08d17-2e3c-4bdb-a7f4-36726cda6ae2", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Tom Moreno (46:06): Yeah, I think consistent communication and alignment. I think honestly, this comes down to your site merchandising managers, your digital product managers, really understanding what their needs are as a stakeholder within the business is that's actually the first step. So it's actually on the site merchandising teams to ask about that because a brand manager, as an example, is thinking across all points of distribution within a given region, and the website is one component of it, maybe a priority, maybe not depending on the category, depending on the brand, et cetera. But really making sure that there's clear alignment on who are the consumers you're targeting for that category. What is the operating best practices that we have for managing our calendar that helps inform what's happening on the website. And where I see probably the most consistent friction, honestly, is really not enough emphasis being focused on the creative brief for say, a campaign. What are the visuals that I need? What are the aesthetics that we need? Because after those are produced, you're playing catch up to try and figure out, okay, I need to tweak this a little bit differently, or this doesn't quite meet my needs. So making sure that everybody's hyper aligned on that creative brief before it goes in across whatever those digital touch points are is super, super important.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:28): So in a perfect world, would you want to see a creative brief before a campaign is put together to weigh input?", "token_estimate": 382, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "46:06", "end_timestamp": "47:28", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "3f067899-f34c-4df0-b8cf-c027bff52623", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 25, "content": "Tom Moreno (47:35): I think it depends on the scale, right? I don't need to see every creative brief, and that doesn't necessarily mean that I as a site merchandising manager needs to see it. Maybe it's just our visual specialist needs to do a checkoff to make sure we're shooting and we have a horizontal banner or a horizontal photo orientation if they were only thinking about social, which may be more vertical. So making sure that we have those little touch points and checkoffs are super important. So I would say it depends. It depends on the scale. I'll use seasonal gift guides as a very current example where again, going back to those alpine differences, it's on peace in Europe and it's free ride oriented in North America. Looking at making sure that creative brief has the ability for either two deliverables that has both those images or the ability to quickly change though, but it's understood upfront as opposed to maybe during the process of those banner generations.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:43): Interesting. So you're starting to get into my world a little bit, and now you're making me wonder, I wonder if we can encourage folks to, we get a creative brief and we assume that it's good to go and all stakeholders have signed off on it. Maybe we could suggest that folks maybe take a deeper dive into their organization if it seems like a project that is going to be touching multiple different components,\n\nTom Moreno (49:09): Understanding how a shoot's expensive, right? I'm sure the quotes are not cheap, and obviously that's going to vary by different production sizes, et cetera, but you're investing the time and effort. You better understand what are my endpoints of distribution where I want this content to be digested. If the website's not part of it, that's fine. That is a brand choice that they can make, but they should still understand is this social, is it, let's use this podcast as an example, right? It's long form, but what are the things that I can chop up within that to use as digital distribution at any given point? So those are key things for marketers to think about.", "token_estimate": 528, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "47:35", "end_timestamp": "49:09", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "d9cdc3e6-dfce-44cf-b6be-0c21b6196936", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 26, "content": "Cole Heilborn (49:50): Well, just as it's important for you to understand what are customers coming to the website looking to experience? What are they looking to find? I mean, it's the same thing when thinking about content. It's where is it going to live? Who's interacting with it? What are their goals? What are we trying to solve? I think everything in life generally comes back to what is the goal and who are we talking to? So we're coming up close on the end of our time for today. This has been super interesting because it's not a perspective that we have a lot of on the show, and I love how we've got a little bit into the weeds of how you think about a site. I've got a few questions for you, but before I run through those, is there anything that we haven't discussed that you think is important to talk about?\n\nTom Moreno (50:36): I would say the other thing that I would consider that it's so easy to forget is we're talking on laptops right now. Your desktop orientation, when you're designing the site or designing a campaign, you have to think about this as your first point of entry for whatever that campaign is. The interfaces are different. There's many more interfaces from specific screen sizes, et cetera, and you have to understand stand again. What is that endpoint of receipt of that content that I'm receiving, and how am I going to get to where I'm going to go? So thinking mobile first is definitely a suggestion I have for everybody. I'm guilty of this. It's so easy. And designing, say a product for the website to think about, okay, this is the orientation on desktop, literally what I'm working in, but my end consumer is probably going to first touch this through the mobile experience, so how do I make that so that when they do go to desktop and they want to convert, I can do that or make it super easy to convert right there on the mobile device.\n\nCole Heilborn (51:41): Yeah, yeah, no kidding. What brands are currently blowing your mind in terms of their design from a site? These could be in the industry or outside the outdoor industry", "token_estimate": 520, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "49:50", "end_timestamp": "51:41", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "47e15dd9-6903-4e75-8102-119b20176df1", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 27, "content": "Tom Moreno (51:54): For sure. I would say outside the industry in particular, if you look at product detail pages, there's basically two types and two companies. So Apple and DJI do a really good job of having very, a good balance of these campaign type product detail pages that meet consumer benefit needs for a more enthusiast user, or they might have something that is extremely technical in what you're looking for. So DJ, as an example, has so many different examples, and I'm speaking your world. These are all the specs, and they know who their end consumer matters, who that matters, what are the specs they're looking for. That translates to, obviously for us, let's use a road running shoe. Maybe it's weight and maybe it's stack height as an example. Apple does the same thing, and I think what I really like about Apple is they make it super immersive. (52:48): DGI does this as well, but what I really like about Apple is let's take a MacBook Pro and let's take an iPad. The end users of those are two different things. For the MacBook Pro, they know that there's a bunch of people using it based on benefits or status, et cetera, but their pro user is who they're really targeting there, and everything else is kind of a trickle down. So they're making sure that the tech specs that they're leading more. So there, I was just looking at an iPad, PDP yesterday, which is highly conversion oriented, but then their campaign version of it is very immersive and very, I would say, much more benefit driven. They still have all the tech specs there for the people that want to look at it, but it is not as technically oriented from a lead perspective as your say, MacBook Pro.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:35): Yeah, I'm thinking about you go to an Apple website and I'm looking at a product, it's a totally, I mean, to use your word, it's a totally immersive experience. You kind of get sucked in. There's that parallax effect as you scroll down and there's subtle animation that's happening. There's a lot of hours that went into designing that.", "token_estimate": 512, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "51:54", "end_timestamp": "53:35", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "cd8515fa-1e62-4bbf-a0e0-3c4a22e25594", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 28, "content": "Tom Moreno (53:53): Yeah, a hundred percent. And that's the benefit of an Apple catalog is their primary, they 20% of skews that are doing 80% of the business, their models are maybe 12 to, let's call it 50 wide. It's not super wide catalog where you may have thousands of SKUs at a brand or say at retail, let's use REI, as an example, has probably, I wouldn't be surprised if they have several hundreds of thousands, if not million plus SKUs with all the little different maps and things that they have. So becomes a much different experience. And again, understanding for whether that's the retailer or the brand, what are my end consumers? Who are my target consumers, and how am I building a website that gives them the experience that they're expecting from me?\n\nCole Heilborn (54:36): Yeah. All right. A last couple just quick questions for you to wrap up. If I don't accept cookies on a website, how much does that affect you and your ability to do your job?", "token_estimate": 242, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "53:53", "end_timestamp": "54:36", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "d92f909b-d3ea-402a-a498-df6ef4e97a86", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 29, "content": "Tom Moreno (54:50): This is actually a really interesting one because in EMEA in Europe, the cookie acceptance rate much, much lower, right? So your ability to personalize and understand who they are is much, much lower. Apple as an example, releasing cookie blocking essentially within their iOS, massively changed the way that people are able to be able to serve targeted ads or targeted even experiences. So I don't have a number for you, but it definitely affects it. It's an interesting thing when you think about just how we have been trained as consumers to just kind of click that box. We can use the app store as the first kind of vector for that, right? Who is going to sit there and read a terms of service or terms agreement? I know you don't. I know I don't. Would be highly surprised if anyone outside of the law industry is actually sitting down and reading these things. (55:46): But they have these massive teams of lawyers that allow you to allow them to have all of the outs for all the different scenarios or the most probable scenarios. So we've been trained and we have kind of like Pavlov's dog. We've just been trained to hit that button to say accept, to move on and move forward because it is a friction point. It's that same popup as when you land on a website and you're given that welcome code for new users. So it absolutely affects your ability to do those things. But at the end of the day, it's your personal needs. If privacy is super important to you, you're probably not going to use Chrome. You're probably going to not accept cookies. Maybe you have a popup blocker. And then on the other side of that, there's people who really want that personalized experience. There's people who some package tracking applications out there that will have access to your email. So they can harvest the tracking number and service you all of the things. But they have access to your email. So if they want, and obviously I wouldn't read the terms of service, but they could read through everything and serve you more targeted ads based on whatever is being said in your email as an example. So there's pros and cons, and you as a person and consumer have to understand what's important to you.", "token_estimate": 555, "speakers": ["Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "54:50", "end_timestamp": "54:50", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "8b39db20-373f-4a0d-aef4-d03e55f3d831", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 30, "content": "Cole Heilborn (57:09): Yeah. Alright. Quickly, are chatbots helpful?\n\nTom Moreno (57:14): That's an awesome question. They can be. They definitely can be. I would say, I don't know if this is a specific call out at ours, but ours needs improvement, but I think everybody's needs improvement. I think a lot of times they're built on trying to solve a specific use case released and then not always necessarily looked as a point of refinement. I think if you look at chat and where it is taking place is really important. Do I have the ability to send you a text message to handle an order? I was just dealing with a website the other day. I order something and then I order something again. I said, Hey, I made two orders. Are you able to wipe off the shipping costs and ship these two things together? They're like, no, I can't do that. But that happened in my iMessage. It didn't happen on a website. (58:03): So making sure that you have those placements within, say, an order confirmation page. Maybe I have a checkbox so I can have order updates or have communication through text message or in other countries, maybe that's WhatsApp, et cetera. So absolutely, but they need to be built to solve your consumer orders. If I can't figure out questions on my orders, you're probably not doing it right to start with, or you're probably not addressing what are your most common issues. And so you have to be able to think through that as far as product recommendations and pieces like that, for sure, that can happen. But at the end of the day, if you walk into a store, is it a robot giving you that recommendation or is it a human? People want that human connection and that ability to have a little bit of back and forth play.\n\nCole Heilborn (58:49): Sure. Makes sense. Well, Tom, we're at the end of our time. If folks want to follow along or connect with you or the brand, is there a place where they can go to connect maybe the website\n\nTom Moreno (59:00): For starters? Yeah, for sure. solomon.com, depending on what country you're in, and you can always find me on LinkedIn. I'm definitely there. Spend way too much time scrolling through. I've got me hooked.", "token_estimate": 542, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "57:09", "end_timestamp": "59:00", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "78f04494-1411-41bb-9c22-864736d181e4", "episode_id": "ebef93de-a705-40d3-8afb-30553f167bfb", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a0", "chunk_index": 31, "content": "Cole Heilborn (59:11): Well, thank you again for taking the time to chat and share your perspective. Like I mentioned earlier, I think this is a really, really interesting conversation and a lot of takeaways. There's a lot of, I feel like we opened up some rabbit holes and we could definitely dive deeper and you could get really deep into the technical side of it all. But you've spared us some of that stuff, so thank you again.\n\nTom Moreno (59:32): Cool. Thank you.\n\nCole Heilborn (59:33): All right. Have a great rest of your day. You\n\nTom Moreno (59:34): Too, Cole.", "token_estimate": 143, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Tom Moreno"], "start_timestamp": "59:11", "end_timestamp": "59:34", "podcast_index": 123, "episode_name": "EP: 123 Website Development for 2023 | Tom Moreno | Salomon", "slug": "ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-123-website-development-for-2023-tom-moreno-salomon", "guest_name": "Tom Moreno", "guest_title": "Salomon Web Development", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Tom joins the podcast to talk about website development, what marketers need to know about websites, user experience, and what trends we should be aware of and following as we head into 2023.", "podcast_length": "60:00"}, {"id": "f3c8b14f-051a-4478-859a-3d27c8ae6105", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Adam Cramer (00:03): I guess for myself personally and for the organization to realize that we had a responsibility to do other things in long-term service. And I guess it's not unlike taking a rest day. You can't pin it every day. And if you do, you're going to get worn out and eventually you're get hurt, and then you're not going to be able to do thing you like to do. So I'm not saying that marketing is like rest, but it's doing something that's not compassion for your initial orientation that's actually in service of compassion.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:46): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo. Today I'm sitting down with Adam Kramer. He's the CEO at Outdoor Alliance. Adam, how are you?\n\nAdam Cramer (00:55): Doing very well. How about yourself?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:57): Doing fantastic. Yeah, having a great day. I'm looking forward to having this conversation with you and diving deep into the weeds. Where are you calling in from?\n\nAdam Cramer (01:07): Washington, DC.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:08): Okay. The other Washington. It's not a lot of, I don't know that we've had any other guests from Washington, DC so you might be the first.\n\nAdam Cramer (01:17): Well, it's an honor.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:22): What do you find yourself up to this week? What's happening in your world?\n\nAdam Cramer (01:28): Well, I don't know. I mean, riding my bike to work, figuring out the world, taking care of my kids and my wife. Trying to get out a little bit, looking at the water level this weekend, it's looking pretty good for the Potomac, so put the spring in my step.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:46): You swimming, kayaking? What are you doing in the Potomac?\n\nAdam Cramer (01:50): Oh, paddling. Yeah. I don't know. I think it's the best whitewater city in the planet. So yeah, we've got the goods here.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:58): I never would've guessed that. Where are your local hotspots?", "token_estimate": 477, "speakers": ["Adam Cramer", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:03", "end_timestamp": "01:58", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "a8827ebe-c921-4d60-969f-81dfdec86a2e", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Adam Cramer (02:04): Well, this is going to knock your socks off, but the Potomac, everybody's got this image of the Potomac floating very serially by all the monuments, but if you go just outside the beltway, it's Class five waterfalls. It's where the Comfort wind plateau meets the peamont. So there's this big geological event and got a lot of water temper climate, big community. Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (02:32): Interesting. Okay, that's awesome. What is the Outdoor Alliance? Can you give us a little background on your organization and what you guys do?\n\nAdam Cramer (02:41): Yeah, so Outdoor Alliance, it's a coalition. We're an NGO. We're made up of 10 member organizations, including the access fund, American Canoe Association, American Whitewater International Mountain Bicycling Association, went to Wildlands Alliance, the Mountaineers, American Alpine Club, the is Colorado Mountain Club and Surf Fry Foundation. We bring together the human powered outdoor community to advocate for protecting public lands and waters.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:11): Gotcha. And how long have you been existence? Did you start the organization\n\nAdam Cramer (03:18): At a pretty heavy hand in it? Yeah, yeah. And we started as an NGO as an organization back in 2014.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:26): Okay. And I know you go on your website, there's a whole bunch of lists of projects that you have worked on. What are some of the milestone projects that you guys have been a part of and what are those protections look like?", "token_estimate": 376, "speakers": ["Adam Cramer", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "02:04", "end_timestamp": "03:26", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "4bba8daa-7091-40fb-9637-e74c8cf1e7b6", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Adam Cramer (03:39): Wow, so many different scales. I give you two at two ends of the spectrum. So one, we passed this massive piece of legislation or help pass this piece of legislation called the Greater America Outdoors Act back in 2020. And it all arises out of this idea of the Land and Water Conservation Fund and dc everybody reduces things to acronyms. So LWCF, so LWCF was started back in the sixties, and this premise on this idea that you've got all this extractive activity across the country, oil and gas minerals. And a lot of times these resources exist on public lands and waters. And the way that it works is that the government will give licenses to companies to extract these resources. And you pay royalties. So you pull out oil and gas, you pay royalty to the federal government because these resources belong to everybody. And back in the sixties, they had this idea that, you know what, some of these royalties we should reinvest in conservation, in recreation, in access for everybody. (04:48): And back then they said, alright, up to 900 million a year could be reinvested back into conservation. And remember, it's back in the sixties, 900 million a year is a lot, but the hitch is that it was up to 900 million a year wasn't a guarantee. Congress was allowed to do it. So every year it's like, alright, how much we going to send to Land and Water Conservation fund? How much good are we going to dispense? And over the existence of the fund, they hit 900 a million I think twice. And there's always this big effort to get as much as you can and where does it go? And so we had the idea along with many others, that it should be 900 million every year. Then we're arguing because the money's already there, let's just send it out and focus on delivering benefits to American people for conservation, make sure it's equitably distributed, and it's something that people were working on for a long time, but eventually political stars lined up in a way that we can make the case. (05:56): So we educated people throughout the community, identified all different opportunities for them to weigh in with their elected officials. Built a massive amount of momentum around this idea. We sensed that the timing was right to do it. And then if at the same time there was a recognition that we, there's a lot of deferred maintenance on public lands. Like you go out to National Forest, your national park and things looking kind of rickety, kind of worn down, lack of investment over time. And then with the pandemic people, lot more people are going outside. So there is an interest in dialing up funding for public lands infrastructure. And the original focus was the parks. So everybody loves National Parks as the saying goes, America's grace, best idea. And we thought as a collective, yes, parks very important, but Forest Service Bureau land management, how about all the other federal management agencies where you've got an enormous amount of outdoor recreation. So we work really hard to say yes for the parks, how hallelujah. You can't cut out Forest Service, you can't cut out VLM. And we worked really hard to expand that investment to include all the federal land Medicaid agencies. So two huge things. A ton of money for land and water conservation, more equitable investment in all public lands and got signed into law, made it happen. So it's a big deal, talking billions of dollars and the ripple effects are going to be experienced and enjoyed for a really long time.", "token_estimate": 876, "speakers": ["Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "03:39", "end_timestamp": "03:39", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "336caaf0-6668-4b5c-9ee8-bfe105f8df58", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (07:44): And that was a part of the Outdoors Act in 2020.\n\nAdam Cramer (07:49): Yeah, both those two things.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:51): So you said you give us one end of the spectrum and another project on the other end of the spectrum. What's the other piece?\n\nAdam Cramer (07:58): Yeah, so oil and gas again, right? Oil and gas leasing happens all over the place. What we do is we recognize that there may be some intersection between development and recreational resources or conservation values. So we monitor the intersection and it came to our attention. There was an oil and gas lease proposed for the area that includes Slick rock Trail in Utah. So one of the most iconic mountain bike resources ever. And BLM was like, huh, let's develop some oil and gas leasing behind Slick Rock. We thought that's not a good idea at all. So it was because we had that awareness that these two things coexist, development and conservation sometimes to conflict. (08:54): We knew about the process, realized it was not a good idea, educated the community, created opportunities for them to weigh in, figured out who the best audience for this was. Wasn't at Maine Interior, but we focused almost exclusively on the state directors in Utah. And people responded with figure and BLM was like, yeah, sorry about that. We're not going to do that. We please forgive us. We didn't realize that. And what's really exciting, Cole, is that based on that, in part BLM evolved thinking about oil and gas leasing and how they plan things out moving forward. And now what they've been experimenting with is a recreational alternative. So instead of doing oil and gas leasing, they're like, well, maybe the best use of land, best use of this parcel is not to develop it at all. Maybe the recreational resources are more important than the oil and gas resources and keep that stuff in the ground. So it's an example of working on something that's very particular and how that can have a ripple effect on national policy.", "token_estimate": 503, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "07:44", "end_timestamp": "07:58", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "466cbbd7-d8db-43b3-b0dd-c4a195da3c25", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:17): So I know here locally in Washington State, our DNR has been having lots of discussions about their trust mandates around logging forests. And for the long time, at least I think in most states, but in our state, the DNR logs, that funding that money then goes towards the trust mandate, which helps fund public education and a variety of other public programs they're now reconsidering or people are suggesting they reconsider. Is it in the best interest for logging to take place or is it in the best interest to keep trees and use them for carbon sequestration instead of cutting them down and sending the money to schools. I'm curious, this conversation with the BLM that's happening about recreation versus extraction, it sounds like a similar conversation that the DNR here is having. Is that a conversation that's happening all across the country? Are these more common these days than maybe we'd think?\n\nAdam Cramer (11:23): Yeah, taking a more nuanced approach to natural resources has been going on for a while and it's in outdoor alliance's interest to dial up that nuance even more. Back in the day, nobody really thought about it. Okay, we need trees, let's cut some down. We need some oil and gas put a well in. We really thought about, I don't know, climate, the disparate impact on communities that might live next to oil and gas well or a transmission line. And the trend towards being a little more sensitive to the nuance of these decisions and being more precise and being more inclusive with, I think other people that are impacted and have an interest, have their say will result eventually in more refined decisions, better decisions. And then you could weigh the relative value of carbon sequestration, which is huge. And ecosystem integrity, which is very, very important with like, yeah, you got to pay for schools too. And so it enables a more refined, more informed decision making, which is on balance of good thing.", "token_estimate": 492, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "10:17", "end_timestamp": "11:23", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "ad3c1a36-9c70-4f5d-acf5-e8018ee77579", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (12:43): So when it comes to your role with the outdoor alliance, I'm imagining you every day out in the field conducting studies, experiencing these places that you're trying to protect. I'm assuming though your day is probably filled with more meetings and paperwork and looking at a computer screen. What exactly does the Outdoor Alliance do or what do you do specifically when it comes to, for example, working on the Great Outdoors Act?\n\nSo we read a ton. It's just (13:16): We spend a lot of time reading and listening and talking, and there's so much going on in public policy and advocacy, not just in conservation, but just for everything. And then within conservation, it's really complicated. So we're just taking stock of what's going on, sorting through all these things, figuring out what's the most relevant to us, what has the most impact on our values? And of those things, how do we find consensus? Because remember, we're a coalition. We've got mountain bikers and surfers and back country skiers and climbers. They don't all see the world exactly the same way. Their priorities are harmonized, they're consistent, but they're not the same. So what's going on? What can we do about it? Can we have an impact from our voice? Can we mobilize the community? It's this incessant evaluation and assessment of how we can make a difference because a mission-driven organization, and then it's that stuff, the opportunities, the things that are important, the opportunities to pursue them, and how do we connect that to the community? (14:40): How do we educate the broader community because people care and they want to be involved and they want to be able to do something. And that's the other part of the day is like, okay, who needs to weigh in and how? What's that going to look like? So engineering or designing a way for the community to be educated and to show up and put some weight in the scale for how decisions are made in Washington DC throughout the country. So yeah, paperwork, not really, I mean, screens sure would zoom, but a lot of reading, a lot of talking, a lot of meetings, yeah.", "token_estimate": 532, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "So we read a ton. It's just"], "start_timestamp": "12:43", "end_timestamp": "13:16", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "57b22516-2ccf-4661-87ce-cbe93a2ce82a", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (15:26): What's one of the proudest moments that you have felt or you've been a part of or your team has been a part of?\n\nAdam Cramer (15:33): Oh wow. Restoring Bear's Ears. That helped set the curve. President Obama designated it in a super thoughtful way at the end of his term, it was put together in such a resilient, compassionate way to protect the landscape and to honor the value to indigenous people in the area. Indigenous governments who were also very gracious about how other people, people value the area. So getting that monument designated and the way it was designated was just this peak experience. And then having that ripped away during the Trump administration was devastating. We fought really hard and having it restored quickly was wonderful. And some of us were invited to the White House for the restoration ceremony, and I was honored to show up there with colleagues that had been thrown down for this designation or restoration for a really long time. So it's very personally gratifying. And I guess the other thing is getting the Inflation Reduction Act signed. Nobody thought that would ever happen, and then it did and put in a huge jet in climate and having in a hand in moving that along, tremendously validating personally for the team. And I'll tell you, Cole was a similar roller coaster. Things were looking pretty good, and then they looked really bad in the summer, and there's a lot of head and hands like, oh my God, but it worked out.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:36): So I got to imagine every day you guys wake up and your sole focus is, as you said, there's so many different layers to what you do, but how do we help incite change? How do we incite protection of these natural places? And I got to imagine if that's your sole focus, that also there's also areas that you probably could grow at or if you're not focusing on them. And I know something that we discussed in our intro call was how does the Outdoor Alliance advocate for itself, or how does the Outdoor Alliance market itself and communicate what you guys do? How do you think through that dichotomy and how does that balance of focus weigh on you?", "token_estimate": 541, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "15:26", "end_timestamp": "17:36", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "094a0be4-28da-40f7-8f39-78ba844f4d23", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Adam Cramer (18:23): It is been an evolution, and I don't think it's peculiar to us at Outdoor Alliance. We're an NGO, we're a mission driven organization. We exist to pursue some pretty lofty goals, pretty consequential goals, and it's heavy. It's a long time horizon. It's difficult work. And we've become, I think exquisite storytellers could spin a yarn. We know how to convert all this policy Golf league good into terms that are accessible for the community. We do a good job, but it takes a lot of time. And I think over the last couple of years realized we are implicitly part of the story, telling the story, but we're not intentionally the subject of the story. We're not the subject of what's going on. We're enabling it. Our fingerprints are all over the place, but we're not front and center. We're not talking about ourselves. And in a sense, you think, well, we're focused on the mission. (19:40): We shouldn't be talking about ourselves. There's so many other things going on. But the realization that we've gone to is that if we don't talk about ourselves at least a bit, there's less people are going to know about us and know about the opportunity for them to get involved. So in a strange way, for us to really most effectively pursue our mission, we have to talk about ourselves. We've got a responsibility to talk about who we are, how we do our work, how people could be involved, the way to scale impact. It's a distribution issue. We've got the goods, we've got the content, and we've been so singularly focused on that. It's time to think about how to get the word out, and if we do, we'll be able to more powerfully pursue our goals. So we've only been around since 2014. I think we're a little bit beyond a startup, but it's time to start thinking about those things. I think it focuses on that realization that actually you do need to talk about yourself and being at peace with that big giant step.", "token_estimate": 482, "speakers": ["Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "18:23", "end_timestamp": "18:23", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "6a59b429-e0db-4e10-8104-420ba80d034e", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (20:59): So if you aren't the subject of the story, but your fingerprints are all over the story, where do you fit into the story? Are you the storyteller? Are you the medium in which the story is being communicated? Are you the voice? Are you the piece of paper? I'm curious to have you continue with that.\n\nAdam Cramer (21:22): Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (21:23): That symbolism.\n\nAdam Cramer (21:23): Yeah. I think we become the subject of the story as we talk about ourselves most directly. So instead of talking about legislation and the community and the subject matter, taking some time to talk about who's telling this story and how do we work? And for example, made up of 10 organizations every week, the policy leads of all those organizations, they meet on a video call called the Joint Policy Shop. So person's thinking about surfing from a policy perspectives, hanging out with the person's thinking about mountain biking and back country skiing. And the fact that that exists is a story unto itself that they're working things out and we're able to forge consensus. How does that even happen? That's kind of interesting and inspiring, and we've got a responsibility to explain who we are, how we work, how it all fits together, and also who we are, our team, what we're based, what rings our bell.", "token_estimate": 330, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "20:59", "end_timestamp": "21:23", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "dcd4411f-7326-494d-9705-e54ab74f2a13", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (22:44): I got to imagine also because of all the research that you guys do, you have a really interesting perspective on these issues at hand. And as you said, oftentimes you're trying to find that intersection of how do we balance all of these needs? And finding that intersection, I think can be really tough, especially for the community who maybe isn't doing all the research, they don't understand all the layers within an issue. But I'm just thinking about the voice that you do have based on all of the research and understanding and the credibility that you have. It almost seems like it would be a shame to not publish or not advocate about those intersections or about those ways to make things work that don't seem feasible. You're trying to bring extraction and conservation together, and at face value, those two seem like they should be at odds, but in some cases they work really well together.\n\nAdam Cramer (23:49): Yeah, they're usually, I mean, it depends on the elevation. They usually are completely at odds. They could coexist sometimes, but I mean, even if you're driving a renewable, an ev, you need metal for the battery, right? I mean, let's, it's a balance, but it's striking that balance in a more sustainable, thoughtful way. That's the trick. But I don't know, help me out with the, I feel like I'm going down one of those rabbit holes right now.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:34): No. Yeah. I don't know if I had a specific question. It was more of just like a thought that was hitting me. It's just up till this point. How do you guys think about marketing and how have you marketed and advocated for yourselves?", "token_estimate": 412, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "22:44", "end_timestamp": "24:34", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "e0edb18b-4889-428c-96a5-134f84ec830f", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Adam Cramer (24:54): I guess in the very beginning of Outdoor Alliance, the relationship to the broader community was through the member organizations themselves, right? American Whitewater, most authentic spokesperson, representative for the whitewater community, ambo for mountain biking. And we thought of ourselves as a beltway entity. So our audience wasn't so much the user community, but policy makers. And there was by design a way to just separate the two. So we would come up with material, the member organizations, and we'd also channel the sensibilities and values of the member organizations and by extension their respective communities into dc. But it wasn't until a number of years ago that we decided that that was a bit myopic and that we could get a whole lot more done by speaking out to the community itself. So it was first recognizing that we had a responsibility to speak to everybody, the entire user community, about the things that we work on as outdoor alliance. (26:10): So that realization was a big deal. And based on that, we developed a whole strategy around email, social media, and all the conventions. But it took us a step to realize that actually that's something we needed to do, that our audience wasn't just people within the beltway people, the Forest Service people in the administration, and that if we could connect to the community at large, we can empower them to lean into policy makers because all these folks are accountable to the public, to constituents. And if we pursue our mission, we have to be able to mobilize people at scale because yeah, does a senator care about what your staff has to say? Yes, they're interested, they take the call, they'll read our letters, but if a bunch of constituents also weigh in and say the same thing or something similar saying It's important to us mountain bikers in New Mexico or Whitewater Boat in Oregon, more weight in the scale. So I guess it's an evolution based on our devotion to our mission and our goals and realizing that a more effective way to pursue those goals is to deal in more people. And the way you do that effectively is you got to explain a bit about yourself. So it's conceptually in the market", "token_estimate": 553, "speakers": ["Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "24:54", "end_timestamp": "24:54", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "bec3121e-b771-4cb5-baff-919371bffe05", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (27:46): At first, did it feel like by focusing on bringing in community members or telling your story to the community, that you were taking away from your mission, that you were not focused on your mission because you are working on something else outside of your sole focus?\n\nAdam Cramer (28:05): I guess given orientation and the type of people that end up on our board and the type of people that work at Outdoor Alliance, there's this orientation towards the mission, towards the mission work. And because the mission work is so complicated, anytime you're stepping away, you're like, oh, there's another thing I should read. There's another person I should talk to. And I guess you got to take a pause and realize, yes, but there's other things that are important. I guess it was a maturation, I guess for myself personally and for the organization to realize that we had a responsibility to do other things in long-term service. And I guess it's not taking a rest day. You can't pin it every day. (29:03): And if you do, you're going to get worn out and eventually you're going to get hurt, and then you're not going to be able to do the thing you like to do. So I'm not saying that marketing is rest, but it's doing something that's not your passion or your initial orientation that's actually in service of your passion. And it took us a little bit of time to come to that realization, and we're still figuring out what to do about it presently making some good strides. But it wasn't because of conscious avoidance, like, no, we don't want to do this.\n\nCole Heilborn (29:48): So what do you see in the future? What are the biggest opportunities for the Outdoor Alliance to continue to advocate your message?", "token_estimate": 433, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "27:46", "end_timestamp": "29:48", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "549c74d0-87bc-4702-b917-14a235b2aa56", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Adam Cramer (29:59): I mean, I think we got an opportunity to rethink conservation at scale. In the beginning, going back to the early part of the conversation, there was almost singular focus on development and extraction everything off, going back to a manifest destiny, let's go out and get everything as much as we can, build as much wealth as we can. And then a reaction to that historically was like, let's make some national parks. Let's come up with the Wilderness Act. Maybe we should have clean water and clean air, like the environmental movement was in part and formed by it like this, I don't know, guilt is the right word or remorse. And there was this focus on restoration of the natural world, protection of the natural world, that there was this swing from development and it's too much, and now we've got to protect it, and there's passion there, but that's, it's not really the most balanced, sustainable way to pursue conservation because people have been here in North America for tens of thousands of years, if you really want to think about it. (31:21): The country's been around as a nation for 250 years and pulling out people and what these places matter doesn't help people and society have got to be incorporated into these protections. And I think that there's an opportunity to evolve how we think about conservation, why we do it, the social aspect of conservation, the value to society of conservative, protected places of intact ecosystems, and using that to inform how we could serve and protect places being afforded. And who has the benefit of those conservations, which is protecting places that are really, really far away. That's great, but how about protecting places where a bunch of people live? How about making sure that people have local parks? And the old style of conservation was singularly focused on the intrinsic value of place. Our vision in After Alliance is thinking about the intrinsic value and the social value of place. And the thinking is that if you articulate social value, you could build more political and social support for those initiatives. And at the end of the day, you protect more places because more people care about it.", "token_estimate": 549, "speakers": ["Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "29:59", "end_timestamp": "29:59", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "3dde59a3-2a9c-4bcf-8f2a-3ae2b4d9fcf5", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (32:48): How do you define the social value or how do you think about social value of a place?\n\nAdam Cramer (32:54): Well, in the experiences and the stories that people have to a given place, why it matters. I care about All Rivers, but I really care about the Potomac, and I care even more about the Great Falls section of the Potomac because I got a connection to that place. And there's meaning infused in places all around, and there's a responsibility to inventory that, to ask to listen, what do places matter to a lot of different communities too. And kind of pulling all that together. And then you'll have a justification or a theory as to why this place matters, who's going to care.\n\nCole Heilborn (33:44): I think I read an article on one of your blog posts or one of the articles on the website, and it was talking about the stigma or the belief that most people feel that politics isn't something that we can affect or isn't something that we really have the ability to incite change. And I think the article went through a bunch of reasons and refuted that and talked about how important it really is that we as constituents reach out to our legislators and voice our opinions. Is there a number of if a senator or representative received a certain amount of correspondence about an issue that it would then be on their radar? Is that a thousand pieces of mail, 10,000 pieces of mail?", "token_estimate": 354, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "32:48", "end_timestamp": "33:44", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "3521740b-a583-4eba-b62d-29be4b756e03", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Adam Cramer (34:29): Yeah. No, it's a positive integer, right? But it's like the input, it all matters, all the input matters, but it's weighted differently based on who it's from. If it comes from a constituent, it matters a lot more than it's coming from somebody who's not a constituent, because these elected officials are accountable to people that put 'em in the office. So not that they don't care about what other people have to say they do, but there's extra witness scale for a constituent if it's an informed constituent, if it's somebody that's explaining from their own personal perspective why an issue is important to them more so, okay, if there's some modicum of understanding of the timing, the urgency, why they're talking about something at a particular time, dial it up even more. And if the lawmaker had not made up his or her or their mind, and they're hearing from the constituent, dial it up to 11, man, it's fluid, but it never doesn't matter. It never doesn't matter. And then there are other ways beyond email, social media, you could show up at a town hall. Now town halls are virtual. So I guess one way to think about it is that if the more time it takes you to show up, think about propose, weigh in, that will be recognized and incorporated into the decision making that whoever you're talking to, whether it's an elected official or it doesn't matter. So it's the number, but the depth.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:40): What are ways that the industry, I mean obviously the industry is full of advocacy support and funding being contributed, but what are some ways that the industry hasn't been thinking about how we can be contributing to conservation efforts? Are there things that aren't being done that could be done?", "token_estimate": 437, "speakers": ["Adam Cramer", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "34:29", "end_timestamp": "36:40", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "3e15c3c8-af09-4fab-982b-2b9c29f2c1f9", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Adam Cramer (37:06): Well, I think it's a joyous question in that the industry, they've got a business interest and then a values interest in conservation. There's an authenticity that is present within the outdoor industry in terms of advocating for conservation. Enviable. A lot of people show up and ask for certain things for a lot of different reasons, but the authenticity that's imbued within the outdoor industry in terms of on these issues is a tremendous asset. So I think that they honoring that orientation is important, and most brands do that. Some of 'em do more and some of 'em do less, but they're all by virtue of what of the industry that they're in, they're all in the game. (38:10): So I think that in terms of supporting advocacy, do one of two basic ways. One is providing support to organizations like Outdoor Alliance and other NGOs and all the member organizations of Outdoor Alliance who are immersed in the game day in, day out, and know what's going on, know what the opportunities are. So providing support for them to just figure out what the opportunities are because brands, and not just to pick on the outdoor industry, who has time for that kind of preoccupied thinking about product and design and sourcing and is like, that's complicated work too. So providing support to NGOs would be great, but also there is a role for them to do advocacy themselves to get into the game themselves, whatever brand there is, they've got at least a Congress person and a couple senators. They've got a governor, they could make themselves heard and learning a bit more about what's important to them and taking a look at opportunities to engage, almost like a menu. You don't have to cook the meal, but take a look at the menu and see what's out there and pick something and show up in terms of direct advocacy, their social media channels, there's so much for them to do. And going back to your question of how many emails does it take? (39:44): An overture from an outdoor brand on a conservation issue is going to matter. And more so if it's to your own elected official. So support and then looking for ways to get in the game themselves. And because there's such a wealth of experience and knowledge within the NGO community, the upper alliance member organizations and us, we could be your guides. You're not going to be wandering around in the universe. We'll show you what the wine is, we'll let you know what you need to bring so you could do it. Have a good time, come home safely.", "token_estimate": 629, "speakers": ["Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "37:06", "end_timestamp": "37:06", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "3776b3b8-905d-49ab-a6a4-79b922347d50", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (40:22): Yeah, I keep thinking about impact and whether that's on the personal level or the brand level or the industry level as a whole. I'm curious to hear your take on, was it five years ago, a few years ago or decided to leave Utah and go to Colorado? There's a number of reasons as to why they explained their reasoning, but I'm curious from an impact perspective and events like that, does that churn things up? Does that create news? Does that create the impact that we would hope for?\n\nAdam Cramer (41:02): Yes. Does create news does create an impact. Its value, I think, is that it reinforces the values of the brands. You care about conservation, you care about protection, and you care about the way the public process to enable those things. You got to, you're standing up for your value, you're standing up for what you believe in. And by not doing that, you're signaling that these things maybe not that important to us, which I think would be devastating to the outdoor brands because it'd be completely contrary to their identity. So I think does it matter? Is it effective? Yes. Is it like a cure all? Does it fix everything? No, it's a little more complicated than that, but at least we're all being honest with ourselves about what's important and we're being consistent with how we present ourselves and the story we tell about ourselves and our actions.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:20): What gives you, I mean obviously you're in this day in, day out, there's good days, there's bad days. What's your general attitude towards the work that we have to do? Are you a fairly optimistic person? Do you choose to be optimistic? Do you feel like you have to be optimistic?", "token_estimate": 423, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "40:22", "end_timestamp": "42:20", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "a2229c4d-a473-45c9-8ebc-aa9dfcf8fe2b", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Adam Cramer (42:43): I'm hopeful and concurrently realistic and honest about the state of the world. There's so many aspects of the world. They're awful and unfair and unjust and embarrassing if you really think about it. And you got to couple that with the realization that there is an opportunity to make things a little better. And it's hard, but it can happen. And because it does happen, I feel a sense of responsibility to keep it a go. That's why it's interesting. If it was a foregone conclusion, everything's going to work out and everything's cool. It's like why bother? It's like the difference between, I don't know, getting on a roller coaster, getting strapped in and letting somebody, letting a Carney pull the lever and then you go on a ride and you're up and you're down. Now, that was a lot of fun. Or going through a really difficult fraught section of sail track where if you screw, you're going to crash. It's way more gratifying to do stuff when there are consequences and it doesn't have to work out. And you roll the dice, that's not the right word. You're not rolling the dice, you're making an informed decision. You're mindful of what the risks are, and it's worth it. You do. It feels good. And sometimes you crash. Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (44:28): Because what is the alternative? That's not a better,\n\nAdam Cramer (44:32): I mean for me, but also just being the essential optimist. I don't think that's especially helpful in this line of work because stuff takes really long sometimes, and it's a circuitous path that we take to being realistic. But being hopeful, coupling those two things together is helps you make some incremental progress sometimes. And that progress is when it happens. It's especially sweet.", "token_estimate": 436, "speakers": ["Adam Cramer", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "42:43", "end_timestamp": "44:32", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "0e632142-c253-4983-b604-28b0ce881dd8", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (45:16): Well, we started this conversation learning about the outdoor alliance and what you guys do, and then we started talking about how important it is for you guys to continue to celebrate what you do and talk about what you do. And I love that we wander deeper into what you guys do because in fact, we are just fulfilling what you said you needed to do, which is talk more about what the alliance does. We're coming up on the end of our time for today, but Adam, if you have any last thoughts, anything to send us home with?\n\nAdam Cramer (45:51): Yeah, everybody that's listening, y'all matter. If you're listening to this podcast, you care about conservation. You care about public lands and waters and what they mean to people, and I'm thankful for that. But use that authenticity for good. Everybody that's listening has got a modicum of power. They can exercise maybe a little more than you realize, and whether you do it with us, their member organizations with your local trail organization, a deciding to dip in and help out at a level that's comfortable for you as an individual, as a business, you're invited. We need you.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:37): If people want to follow along or get connected with you or the outdoor alliance, where can they go to connect?\n\nAdam Cramer (46:42): Well, we're on pretty much every platform. Our website is probably, that's the treasure trove of all the stuff with all the details that you want and all the opportunities we reach out to the community judiciously. When there's stuff to do, we'll let you know. There's stuff to learn, we'll let you know. So following us on social, getting on the email list would be great. And yeah, Dora will be cracked open to get involved in the game.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:20): Well, Adam, as we're approaching the end of our episode, I'm curious if there are any other landmark conservation efforts underway that we should all be aware of that we could learn about.", "token_estimate": 491, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Cramer"], "start_timestamp": "45:16", "end_timestamp": "47:20", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "e3eeab63-8de3-4e8d-8f22-449ba8ca6888", "episode_id": "8b5365a5-4e01-442c-98f7-5cf4f0b99dc4", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a1", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Adam Cramer (47:32): Yeah, certainly. Well, there'll be an election in a few weeks, as you know. And then what happens after an election, you've got what's called a lame duck session of Congress where everybody's laser focused on getting a bunch of stuff done and legislation that's been kind bumping around all Congress. It's finally like Go time. And this La Duck, we're anticipating opportunities to advance conservation and recreation efforts on a couple of fronts. One that's especially promising. It's called the America Outdoor Recreation Act. It's bipartisan, it's building momentum, it's going to deliver the goods for the human power community and beyond. And there's also building momentum to conserv, conserve and protect hundreds of thousands of acres of wilderness and thousands of miles of wild scenic rivers through protective designations. And they cobble things together at the end of a lane duck and having some rock solid outdoor recreation policy and the prospect for some permanent protections across the country, super promising. So after the election, get ready to dip in and help move these things across the line. The opportunity will definitely be present by the end\n\nCole Heilborn (48:45): Of the year. Okay, awesome. Where can we go to stay conscious of what's happening with that?\n\nAdam Cramer (48:53): Best single place is the Outro Alliance website. All the member organizations are also hip to what's going on as well, but we're on social media channels and if you really want to get the latest and when to dip in, being on our email list is the thing to do for sure.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:12): Awesome. Well, Adam, thank you again for taking the time to chat. This has been a lot of fun and super insightful into your organization, the day to day that you guys are working on. So I really appreciate it and wish you guys the best of luck as you're heading into the lame duck season.\n\nAdam Cramer (49:29): Thank you, Cole. It was a pleasure. I enjoyed our conversation.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:32): Alright, well have a great rest of your day.\n\nAdam Cramer (49:34): Likewise.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:34): Bye.", "token_estimate": 533, "speakers": ["Adam Cramer", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "47:32", "end_timestamp": "49:34", "podcast_index": 124, "episode_name": "EP: 124 Why Conservation Depends on Storytelling | Adam Cramer | Outdoor Alliance", "slug": "ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-adam-cramer-why-conservation-depends-on-storytelling-outdoor-alliance", "guest_name": "Adam Cramer", "guest_title": "Founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance", "categories": [], "short_description": "Adam Cramer is the founder and CEO of the Outdoor Alliance. At the Outdoor Alliance, they mobilize the power of outdoor enthusiasts to protect public lands and waters. As Adam calls it, \"conservation powered by outdoor recreation, so to speak.\"", "podcast_length": "50:16"}, {"id": "8a8d3dd5-79d7-47ed-a0d2-1f409515c7be", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Liv Faris (00:00:00): I think it's always a balance of when do we do it and when do we bring in a partner, but seeing more and more opportunities for us to do it a little bit differently and also have a couple people on my staff now that are more used to doing things that way too. And so I think that will help us shift it a little bit.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole, he born. Today I'm sitting down with Leaf Ferris. She's the senior manager of creative content Production over at Brooks Running Leaf. How are you?\n\nLiv Faris (00:00:43): Good, how are you?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:45): I'm doing great. Looking forward to sitting down and chatting with you today.\n\nLiv Faris (00:00:49): Yeah, thanks so much for having me on the podcast. This is going to be fun.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:53): Absolutely. You were just telling me before we started rolling, this is your first podcast, so congratulations. It's a big moment.\n\nLiv Faris (00:01:00): Yeah, it's pretty fun being asked to do this. I really appreciate it. Done lots of other kinds of interviews in my time, but first podcast, so first time for everything.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:11): Where are you calling in from?\n\nLiv Faris (00:01:12): I'm in Seattle, north Seattle, New York Lake, and a beautiful lake just around the corner where we can walk to and enjoy a little bit of snow this morning, but still beautiful\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:25): Is Brooks's HQ out of Seattle,\n\nLiv Faris (00:01:30): Our headquarters in Fremont, just down by Lake Union, New York Gas Work Park and right by the Burt Gilman Trail, which is great for our staff and runners to be able to get out there and get some exercise.", "token_estimate": 429, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:00:00", "end_timestamp": "00:01:30", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "c1ae00bd-e1ee-4dbe-b343-ac7f7e6a5713", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:01:44): Beautiful. Well, Liv, I'm really excited to have you on the show and we're going to dive into a really fun topic that's very near and dear to my heart and it's sounds like it's very close to your heart as well. We're going to be talking all about content and video content and when to bring video content. Love. If you could give us a quick synopsis of your job with Brooks, the department that you oversee, and I know there's a lot of change happening in the department, but we're going to get into some of that in the conversation\n\nLiv Faris (00:02:12): For sure. So I started at Brooks in February of last year, right in the midst of the pandemic, and I lead our creative content production team. So it's a team of producers and we work with our marketing partners to do video and photo content, everything from sales content for our sales meetings all the way through to social media, content wide range of stuff.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:02:39): And how long have you been with the company?\n\nLiv Faris (00:02:41): I've been there a little over almost two years now. I guess coming up in February we'll be the anniversary.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:02:48): Gotcha. And with your job description, what's your day-to-day look like? What do you do?", "token_estimate": 316, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:01:44", "end_timestamp": "00:02:48", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "8d8af43a-15b2-4eb1-ba7b-4eb49d2c0177", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Liv Faris (00:02:54): Yeah, so I oversee on our team, there are 10 of us on our team right now just getting granted to hire two more. So we've been growing quite a bit. And just to oversee the day-to-day of all of the work that we do, which is again, video and photo. We work with our partners, our copywriters, designers, project managers, and within our creative studio to create content. So overseeing those producers, making sure that they have what they need, making sure projects are running smoothly, resourcing budgets, invoices, contracts, all the fun stuff that comes along with production. And then every once in a while I get to go out on a shoot as well. I just got back from a trip to Portugal where we were shooting some trail running, which was pretty fun. So I get to do a project every now and then, but most of the time it's just supporting our team of producers that are super busy with lots on their plate, making sure they have everything that they need.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:04:00): How did you get into the production world? What about it interests you?\n\nLiv Faris (00:04:04): Yeah, so I started out in journalism, always had an interest in journalism, was doing some print journalism and then had an opportunity to work at a TV station in San Francisco. And so that was my first sort of dipping my toe into production and it got in my system and I think anybody that production that does production knows that that can happen. And so from there, my career just evolved from working in broadcast television and then as a producer there and then working at an agency where I built a content team and production team there. And then did a little bit of a turn at Expedia and then ended up at Brooks where they were looking for somebody to lead the production team and felt like a great opportunity to work for a brand that was really meaningful and doing good in the world and just continue my love for production and wanting to be on set and solve problems, all of the things that come along with it.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:05:11): I'm curious, I feel like we could probably swap some great production horror stories and we won't go\n\nLiv Faris (00:05:17): There,", "token_estimate": 545, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02:54", "end_timestamp": "00:05:17", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "fa339b95-ccd7-49c5-85fc-e7ef6b117bb6", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:05:18): But what is your favorite and your least favorite part about production or just projects in general? Video projects?\n\nLiv Faris (00:05:25): Oh, good question. I mean, I would actually say that they're kind of one in the same. Like I said, we were just on the shoot in Portugal with a couple of our athletes. We definitely had some challenges. One of them got hurt, we had a drone fall out of the sky, things went wrong. But I just think that comes with the territory and I think while that's not a great part of the job, it's also part of the job that I really love because I do really well in stress and moments like that of keeping a clear head and looking ahead to what needs to happen next. So I love solving problems and like I said, do well sort of in crisis. So I think it's a two-sided coin of it's never great when something goes wrong on set, but at the same time I feel like that's one of my strengths. So it feels good to be able to help solve those problems and move things forward.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:06:27): So you thrive in the situations where most folks don't want to be.\n\nLiv Faris (00:06:31): Yeah, I would say that's true. Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (00:06:35): Sounds like you're in the right job. And on that shoot in Portugal, what was your role on set? Just so folks maybe have an idea of what are you doing while you're actually out on location?", "token_estimate": 348, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:05:18", "end_timestamp": "00:06:35", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "fbfd5357-ecd5-42cd-a11a-11c33681755c", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Liv Faris (00:06:44): So on that particular project, I was the producer for the brand producer and not just there as head of production, but actually producing. And so we were working with a production company on that project. Also had our in-house photographer was there as well. And yeah, my job was just to make sure things were going smoothly, be there to solve problems as they might come up, support our athletes, which is really important for us when they're on set and taking time out of their careers to do this work with us. And yeah, I really just want to make sure that the crew is happy, that everybody feels included, everybody's getting fed well, and then just having fun too. So in this case, part of my role was when some of these things got a little challenging to work with our internal team that was there as well as our production company producer to figure out, okay, what do we do next now that something's going differently than we expected?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:07:58): Sure. So we're recording this December 1st, which I didn't realize until I logged into the computer this morning\n\nLiv Faris (00:08:06): December already.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:08:07): I know. So when this episode comes out, we'll obviously be a little past the first few weeks, but as we're moving into 2023, I always feel like at the end of the year I always see those HubSpot year in review data trends or predictions for the next year. And I'm curious what you're seeing in the marketing world and just in terms of video trends or video needs that might pop up, how is Brooks looking at the changing marketing video needs of 2023 and how are you thinking through that?", "token_estimate": 416, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:06:44", "end_timestamp": "00:08:07", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "247f5884-ea12-4c72-9781-c270c1ca0a6e", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Liv Faris (00:08:46): Yeah, I think for us social is a big part of it. I think it has been for a while within the last we started on TikTok, so we're doing more of that. And I think for us at Brooks, it's really a variety of video content that we're doing across the board. And so it's really looking at the campaigns that we're doing, what makes the most sense from a video perspective and a photography perspective, how are you looking at the whole campaign? And just trying new things I think is part of it, trying to learn from what we've done in the past and what's going to best position our brand and our product in the content that we're making. And so I think one of the things going into next year is that we'll be working more closely on some of our work with our influencer and creator teams. We're going to be working more closely together. So I think that's the next thing I see for our team is understanding that and how do we do that well together. And then also this in-house, which I know we're going to talk more about is how do we do more in-house versus using vendors and production partners. I think there's always a time for both, but those are kind of the two big things for us moving into next year that I see for my team.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:10:18): You took the plunge into the TikTok,\n\nLiv Faris (00:10:21): Took the plunge into the TikTok. Yeah, I think it's going well. We don't sit within the social team, so that's a different team. We have a social agency that's helping us with some of that content, but that was a big decision for us last year.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:10:34): So I'm curious how the in-house team is structured, and we don't need to get into specific details, but where do most of your requests for work? Are you set up a studio inside of the Brooks umbrella? Is that how it works?", "token_estimate": 457, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:08:46", "end_timestamp": "00:10:34", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "8bb8cd98-1dec-4eba-8d6c-3cd0a7ff6481", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Liv Faris (00:10:48): Yeah, so our team sits in the creative studio, which sits within our marketing organization. So ultimately we report up to our CMO. We have a VP of creative that leads our creative studio and our studio is growing. And over the last few months we've really come to see that the best work we can do for the brand comes from our in-house talent and team. And so we're building up those capabilities. So that was a decision point a few months ago that was made and now we're really trying to build more of an in-house agency is how we're looking at it. And with that comes need for process and staff and all of that. And so in terms of how the work comes in, that's one of the things that we're working on is figuring out how do we get all of the work to come in through one sort of point of understanding everything versus coming in from multiple places. And so yeah, briefs come in, we review the briefs and then we resource from there.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:12:04): Very interesting. So in preparing for this interview, I found a statistic that I think kind of tees us up to segue into our conversation is that this came HubSpot and it said 37% of companies exclusively create video content in-house, 14% rely on an agency and 49% share a mix of both in-house and outside agency help. So I'm curious if you could kind of describe just the brief timeline and history of the studio. I think a lot of folks when they think about creating a studio or an in-house team, they assume they have to go from zero to 100 in a year. And I know for you guys that hasn't been the case and you're still growing and you're still trying to figure out how to evolve. But could you describe just the overall progression of your team?", "token_estimate": 434, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:10:48", "end_timestamp": "00:12:04", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "cc40bfc2-843b-4038-964e-092a1af0398e", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Liv Faris (00:12:48): Yeah, I mean I think creative has been a part of Brooks work for a while, and we have people on our studio team that have been at the company for, I can think of one designer who's been here for eight years. I have producers on my team that have worked for Brooks for 13, 14 years and have evolved in their role over time. So I think the studio has grown. There was just recently actually someone shared a PowerPoint that was done I think maybe four or five years ago, maybe not even that long, that showed who was on the creative team and it was maybe 15 people, something like that. I mean small enough that everybody had their photo and a little blurb and then now our team is 50 or more. So I think in my time at Brooks, it's grown dramatically. Part of that is given our decision to bring more of this, create an agency and just the amount of work has grown that the company has grown in our revenue and with the increased revenue comes increased marketing dollars. (00:14:00): So there's more that we're doing with that. And so yeah, it's just constantly growing. It can sometimes take a bit for companies I think to really reach that tipping point where it goes over into sudden growth. And I think that's one of the challenges too, is how do you maintain the culture of what it was When I know when I was at an agency, we all went to lunch together and then it got to the point where we couldn't do that anymore because we would take up the whole restaurant. And so that was kind of sad. So I think there's a culture shift as you grow from being small to being big, but there's also the benefits that come along with that as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:14:48): So when a brand is trying to go out and create video content, historically there's been kind of two paths. Either you do it in-house, you hire folks or you hire someone externally, a studio, a production company videographer to help you with that project. I'm curious, how does Brooks look at that question and how have you started? I'm curious just to look at that question on a timeline and start to understand how is your perspective changing and why are you leaning towards this in-house approach?", "token_estimate": 548, "speakers": ["Liv Faris", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:12:48", "end_timestamp": "00:14:48", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "5c03f4a0-bb4f-44e8-b00a-c329c23e2f52", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Liv Faris (00:15:23): Yeah, so when I started at Brooks, I mean definitely the way that the production team has functioned has been that our producers have been in more of a supervisory role where they're hiring vendors to implement, to line produce, to do all of that work. And I think part of it comes from my background as well of wanting to shift that and then also the needs of the business needing to be more efficient, more flexible. And so I think when I started, there was a lot of fundamental things with the team that we wanted to work on of just as the company was growing process and all of those things. And so to sort of shake up this, let's do it more as prevalent as maybe it is now or the opportunity wasn't as great as it is now. And so yeah, so I just think as more and more work is coming our way and more and more asks are being made of us, it's just making me think about it a little bit differently of, Hey, can we look at this a different way? (00:16:36): I've had producers that have been more in a line producing capacity. I think it's always a balance of when do we do it and when do we bring in a partner? But I just seeing more and more opportunities for us to do it a little bit differently and also have a couple people on my staff now that are more used to doing things that way too. And so I think that will help us shift it a little bit. I think our first real foray into this, I guess was we hired associate producer that also had some photography skills, and so we said, well, let's offer up those skills to the team and make that available. And we saw right away that that was valuable and helpful to our teams. So he splits his time between producing and photography. So for example, on this Portugal shoot that I was just on, he was helping me produce the project, but also had the added benefit of bringing his camera along and taking some photos while we were there versus having to hire another outside photographer to come and do that.", "token_estimate": 495, "speakers": ["Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:15:23", "end_timestamp": "00:15:23", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "89efaa14-88d7-457c-8c26-ba6bb6973d86", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:18:03): So you said you started to notice something that maybe you could do something differently or approach things differently. What are some of the benefits that you have found by doing things differently? By bringing folks?\n\nLiv Faris (00:18:16): Yeah, I think one of the big things that I see is that people just the ownership and the pride and the passion for the work, when you're doing it more yourself, you just have more ownership and more pride in what you've done. And I think that comes through in the work. So for example, how we got connected was through a video that I shared that we did with the Snoqualmie tribe around respect for the land that you're running on. And that was something that people invested their heart and soul into that this was an important point for us as a brand to support that we're running on this land and we want to respect that and it's not just about conquest. And that was something where our team really needed to understand that and communicate that and feel that. And so that was a really good example of I think, where being able to fully invest their time and thought and have that pride in what they're doing came through in the work.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:19:33): Yeah, that's a great point. Any work that has a strong emotional attachment, it seems that it's so important for the team that's working on it to be as emotionally invested in the project because the story is told through the lens of the people who are actually making it. And if they're not a hundred percent committed, the story probably isn't being communicated as well as it could be. So you've seen stronger ownership and that has led to stronger emotional responses or a stronger emotional project because of that.", "token_estimate": 439, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:18:03", "end_timestamp": "00:19:33", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "a05f7c05-d898-45a4-be47-f5b947d957e4", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Liv Faris (00:20:07): Yeah, I think it comes through in the photos, it comes through in the work and it comes through in the sharing of the work. It's not just about winning awards. Winning awards is great, we love that, but it's, Hey, look at what I worked on. Look at what I did. I'm really proud of this. I think that that's a big part of it. I think there's efficiencies that come with it. So I'll give an example of a recent thing that we did that has a little bit of both. So we were wanting to do some content for Halloween, and so there was some ideas being thrown around our shoe, which is the ghost, and we were launching the ghost, and this is a great opportunity to tie it to Halloween. And so there was a challenge put forward to, Hey, let's make some content and let's do this all in-house, right? (00:21:03): And so were able to, our goal is eventually to have more of a studio and kind of build out the capabilities, but right now we have a small photo room. And so we were able to have our in-house photographer, one of our producers, one of our designers work together and pulled off this, it was photography of a cauldron with a shoe levitating above it and the smoke and all of that Halloween vibes, and we shared that piece of content generated sales, some really good numbers. I mean, metrics is part of the work that we do. (00:21:54): Ultimately, we're a brand that's supporting runners and giving runners the gear that they need and letting them make purchasing decisions about what's going to be best for them. And so this was a chance for us to share our shoe and people saw this content and decided to buy it. And so that's great. And so I think there was a note that came from my producer on that project to me that she said, Hey, we just got these numbers. Take a look at how this did. And I love getting emails like that from my team. I got another one recently, Hey, I'm really proud of this. I wanted to share it with you. I don't always see it first. They sometimes see at first. So I just think that Halloween example was a good one of they spent a couple days, they made this cool thing, they shared it out, it had a impact on the business. They were proud of what they did. And so I just want us to do more things like that. Not to say we're not proud of work that we do with production companies too. Absolutely. I just think there's a little extra level of pride for the team when they're doing it themselves that way.", "token_estimate": 615, "speakers": ["Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:20:07", "end_timestamp": "00:20:07", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "54502d38-9765-402d-a59b-f806b44fd131", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:23:12): Well, you're talking about efficiency, and I'm curious to hear your thoughts on the opportunity cost of a timely piece of content. If you go outside, maybe it takes longer. Well, chances are it's going to take longer than it would if you could just pull it off. So there's that gap between when we can get this asset live and there's sometimes a revenue number attached. If it's not known, there is some sort of revenue number attached to that gap in time. How do you see that play out through the efficiency side of it all?\n\nLiv Faris (00:23:45): Yeah, I mean I think there's always going to be give and take. Pulling a photographer and producer for a day or two to go shoot a cauldron in our photo room is going to take away from other work, right? It's going to happen, but I think it's on our own schedule and our own time and we're able to really evaluate that easier, I think, than relying on a partner. So for example, yesterday I was working on the video we shot in Portugal. I was working on a schedule for post because of some of the things that happened on the shoot, we've had to shift a little bit in the content itself. And so we've had to adjust our post schedule. And so I was working on a schedule yesterday to send back to our production company, but I have to take into account what's happening on their end too. (00:24:41): I don't know what their other projects are. I don't know what else they have on their plate versus if it was an in-house editor for example, which we don't have right now, but did we have that? I would know everything that's on that person's plate. And I would say, okay, if she's going to work on this, then she's not going to be able to work on this. And so I think there's always that give and take. Sometimes we're resource, we don't have the resources, so the only way to get it done is, Hey, I need to bring in a freelancer to do that, or I need to hire a production company to do that. So I think it's a constant give and take in what's going to be the most efficient thing and what's going to be the best thing for that piece of content.", "token_estimate": 529, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:23:12", "end_timestamp": "00:23:45", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "d676d6a8-292f-4a9f-83aa-33ce67374123", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:25:28): Sure. I'm curious if we could dive into the cost perspective because cost is often another reason that people bring up actually pro and con of bringing things in house sometimes I've heard it's cheaper to outsource it sometimes I've heard it's cheaper to bring it in house. How do you think through the financial side of it all?\n\nLiv Faris (00:25:46): Yeah, I feel like this is a constant battle because there's no A ICP, the producers organization has contracts and all of this. They have things that help guide us, but there's not a rate sheet that is used across, I mean, it's like any business, right? Supply and demand impacts, rates and so forth. So I think in production over my career, I've seen everything from the one man band that is willing to do it for a lower cost to the 50 people on set crew that costs way more. And so I think it's no standard to go off of. And so I think it's just a matter of taking it project by project and saying, okay, what the most sense for this? What does the cost look like? We try to do triple bids so that we are getting a good sense of what costs are. (00:26:53): Is that something that we can do that we'll have a time cost? Because like I said before, it's going to, doing one thing is going to take away from doing something else, so it's not necessarily free. I just actually had that happen. I got an email from a stakeholder that was asking about something that they needed shot, and I said, Hey, that's potentially something that we could do in house. And then they said, oh, does that mean it's going to be free? And so yes, it will reduce costs because I don't need to hire an outside photographer. I don't need to pay a licensing fee, that kind of thing. But we still potentially need props and we may still need talent and there's time for Gavin to shoot that. So it's not ever going to be free. It's just weighing the pros and cons or the costs of the things and what makes the most sense for that project.", "token_estimate": 498, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:25:28", "end_timestamp": "00:25:46", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "c4b978e9-1a36-4432-9e98-d9f3f3a2f166", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:27:56): Gotcha. So you take it very project approach and you say, this project makes sense in-house. This one might make sense outhouse, if you could summarize or just generalize the types of projects that are better suited for your team in-house, just so folks maybe have an idea of what actually works for you guys.\n\nLiv Faris (00:28:14): Yeah, I mean that's what we're figuring out right now because we don't have a lot of in-house capability. I've mentioned our photographer, so we found having him go and shoot events, going and doing some of these more in the moment type things definitely makes sense. I had a request this week for do we have any photos that don't have a licensing timeline on them? A lot of 'em are two years. And so is this something where it's better for us to have that complete ownership over things? Sorry, I lost my train of thought.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:28:59): No, continue with that. But I'm just curious, what type of deliverables does the in-house team, what are they really capable of? What do you guys thrive on? And then what are examples of projects you might outsource?", "token_estimate": 282, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:27:56", "end_timestamp": "00:28:59", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}, {"id": "6fa03940-19d8-4d57-b1db-38c96a97aa8e", "episode_id": "eb167414-4f86-4af4-a9c7-d195d5887f60", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c48a5", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Liv Faris (00:29:11): Yeah, so I think the Halloween example is a great one where if it's a simpler, and I don't want to downplay it in saying it was simple because it was complex in its own way, but there wasn't 10 talent and there wasn't a huge set. And so that made sense. Can we do this ourselves? It seemed like we could. And then there's the ones that are more complicated, lots and lots of talent that maybe where we need some additional support. So I think right now as we're growing our team, that's just where we need to look at the different things that are coming in and say what makes sense here. And as we build out the team, one of the things that I'm seeing is that we need a in-house videographer editor. So on a project right now, we're actually trying out having an editor work separately from our production company on a certain portion of the deliverables. (00:30:16): So for example, paid media, that's one thing. When you ask about trends and things like that, we're being asked to do more and more paid content versus organic content cut downs for Facebook, Pinterest. The more social sites we have, the more need that we have for something that's tailored to that site. And it's not always new content, it's just versions of this needs a logo, this doesn't. Things like that. So that's one thing we're trying out right now is we have the production company that's doing the bulk of the deliverables, but we had this other set that needed to get done and we said, could we do that in a more cost effective way? So we're trying it out. So we've got a freelance editor thinking of that person as in-house editor. If we were to have that, what's it like for my producer to manage both the production company and the editor versus just the production company? (00:31:15): What's the cost? When we were thinking about doing that, okay, production company, how much is it going to cost for you to make these, okay, freelance editor, how much is it going to cost for you to make these? And then weighing that and seeing, okay, it's actually more cost effective to have this, but then give and take. My producer is going to have to manage these two entities now. And what is that like? So it's test and learn all the time. I feel like try it out, see what works, learn from it, move on. Not everything's going to work. Some things are going to work better than you thought and some things are going to be just okay. But that's how you learn and you figure out what you need.", "token_estimate": 623, "speakers": ["Liv Faris"], "start_timestamp": "00:29:11", "end_timestamp": "00:29:11", "podcast_index": 125, "episode_name": "EP: 125  When to Build an Internal Production Studio | Liv Faris | Brooks Running", "slug": "ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-124-when-to-build-an-internal-production-studio-liv-faris-brooks-running", "guest_name": "Liv Faris", "guest_title": "Content Production at Brooks Running", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "In this episode, Cole and Liv discuss when to bring your content production in-house, who and when to outsource your content, how to build a business case to create an internal studio, and much more.", "podcast_length": "1:02"}]
